window.harnessChunks["rows-0e04ad9e72e6a6e9.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T04:18:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RanaPriyansh/agent-loop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-loop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RanaPriyansh/agent-loop/tree/c4c5dcd35bf89a36b6b477f4d9cb66c4b838a4eb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c4c5dcd35bf89a36b6b477f4d9cb66c4b838a4eb\">c4c5dcd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce and iteratively revise a local code repair to satisfy a selected test suite. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing tests, evolving diagnostics after a patch, unexpected current file content and repeated errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in explicitly selected LLM mode, choose corrected code based on test diagnostics and file content; the harness writes it and returns fresh test output to the next planning step. - Decision owner: model-assisted Planner within one Agent loop in --use-llm mode; the operator establishes the task boundary and provider access. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded eight-iteration scheduler, allowlisted test commands/paths, test feedback, target-file heuristic, JSONL event log and early-stop guards. - Closure path: selected test command → failure output → target file and current code → optional model-selected repair → allowlisted file write → rerun tests → further repair or stop. - Boundary reachability: cli.py explicitly wires --use-llm to Agent(usellm=True), whose constructor calls Planner(usellm=True) and whose run calls planfix; this selects planwithllm with the documented separate SDK/key prerequisites. No unregistered tool or external orchestration layer is required for this branch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-selected correction is open-ended task-specific discretion inside a first-party feedback loop; the default regex-like arithmetic rewrites alone would be a non-agentic fixed script. Harness guardrails and remote inference do not establish another independent VSM unit. - Evidence: cli.py; agent.py; planner.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural; 14 first-party deterministic tests passed, with no live model invocation. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped optional-mode reachability; unmeasured for production LLM repair performance. - Caveats: no Anthropic SDK/key or live model was used; script-only tests validate deterministic behavior and the absent-key guard, not model decision quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party interference-damping coordination among distinct repair S1 units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent changes or competing agent commitments are outside this one-loop distribution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none over interference between independent S1 agents. - Decision owner: one Agent sequentially runs tests, planning and writes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: a sequential bounded iteration counter, file allowlist and subprocess restrictions. - Closure path: no distinct S1 population → S2-specific detection/coordination → behavior correction circuit is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordering one agent&#39;s steps and restricting filenames is not cross-S1 damping. - Evidence: agent.py; tools.py. - Basis: full small-source structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream users could separately coordinate multiple processes; that coordination is not included here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current multi-unit resource, priority or commitment management is established at a metasystem recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: test failure and iteration exhaustion are task-local issues, not system-wide agent-capacity imbalances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no higher-level current management decision over an agent population. - Decision owner: user supplies initial task/boundaries; deterministic Agent guards stop or continue one loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MAXITERATIONS, fixed per-test timeout, repeat-error stop, allowlisted paths/commands and audit logs. - Closure path: test output changes the local repair sequence or terminates it; there is no whole-current S3 view → management decision → returned allocation/intervention path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: resource caps and stop rules constrain one S1 and are not current metasystem discretion, including parent-governed S3. - Evidence: agent.py; cli.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator-provided workspace/paths and test commands do not supply an operational S3 parent decision return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate reality-checking audit path with a corrective return independent of ordinary repair reporting is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tests observe the immediate artifact and the log records activity, but neither supplies complementary organizational audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent audit finding or escalation owner. - Decision owner: the same loop reads test output and appends log records. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tests, testrun and fixapplied JSONL events, and unit tests in the development repository. - Closure path: ordinary test failure → same repair planner; no independent claimed-status challenge → separate management corrective return is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: running tests as an operational success gate is not complementary audit; append-only logging does not make an auditor. - Evidence: agent.py; tests/testagent.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an outside auditor could consume JSONL, but none is packaged as a first-party return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective environmental scanning and strategic capability adaptation is closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: test errors and current code are used for immediate fixes, not to forecast changing external conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no future-oriented capability selection or policy change from external distinctions. - Decision owner: user picks planner mode and goal in CLI; the planner selects a current file patch. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional LLM code fix, static mode switch, tests and log. - Closure path: failure evidence feeds present S1 repair; there is no outside/future sensing → alternatives → adaptation decision → implementation/return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an LLM-generated patch is current operations, not strategic adaptation of the harness. - Evidence: planner.py; cli.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repairing downstream code may help future tests, but does not establish an S4 decision owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate first-party decision path for ultimate organizational identity, purpose or policy is implemented. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially unsafe commands/paths are checked under fixed enforcement rules, not resolved as ultimate-policy questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user determines initial task and permitted files; no shipped S5 deliberation and authoritative return. - Decision owner: external operator/maintainer for goals and configuration; script enforces fixed constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: command allowlist, path checks, max-iteration cap, API credential requirement and CLI flags. - Closure path: prescribed safety settings gate local operations; no identity/policy issue → legitimate deciding authority → durable system-governance return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission/command checks and task selection are not ultimate organizational policy governance. - Evidence: tools.py; cli.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository maintainers retain real-world governance outside the runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6115f17087ed48a6783c32d5348dcc4f4e5380f1/assessments/ra/ranapriyansh-agent-loop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6115f17087ed48a6783c32d5348dcc4f4e5380f1\">6115f17 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T18:08:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KhanUzeb/kite\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kite</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KhanUzeb/kite/tree/1a8b7feafb3661e688e48e5b1923c6390b443d78\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1a8b7feafb3661e688e48e5b1923c6390b443d78\">1a8b7fe &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute open-ended local software-engineering work using model-selected tool actions, inspection and observation-dependent continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown repository content, test failures, command output, edit consequences and changing task state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next model action, tool arguments and whether to continue or finish the engineering task. - Decision owner: model-driven Kite agent operating inside the first-party DefaultAgent turn loop; external inference is a provider dependency, not an external prebuilt coding-agent decision workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Harness and AgentRuntime setup, policy gates, tool registry/execution, parallel-tool conflict partitioning, user approvals, budgets, session/checkpoint state and verification collector. - Closure path: operator task → DefaultAgent.run → query → executeactions → formatted tool observations added to messages → next model turn or final submission. - Boundary reachability: the ordinary shipped CLI/runtime instantiates this first-party model/tool continuation loop; it is not limited to tests, examples or benchmarks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: next-step tool selection is model-directed rather than predetermined by a static script; first-party policy limits tools but does not replace the agent&#39;s task discretion. - Evidence: src/kite/agent/loop.py, src/kite/agent/harness.py, src/kite/agent/runtime.py, src/kite/tools/coding.py. - Basis: explicit first-party source. - Confidence: high for code structure and reachable path; no live provider-backed execution observed. - Caveats: actual results and permissible actions vary with provider, mode, operator grants and installed plugins.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately evidenced regulation of a specific interference or oscillation among distinct operational S1 units at the selected session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool write collisions and subagent work-scope violations are possible, but the observed mechanisms prevent unsafe overlap or reject out-of-scope results without establishing an S2-specific inter-S1 coordination judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an actor would need to arbitrate a substantive interaction disturbance between distinct S1s and return that result to their future work; this right is not established in the inspected shipped paths. - Decision owner: planexecutionbatches deterministically serializes overlapping tool calls; child-task orchestrator accepts task scopes and enforces them, while the main agent can request child work. Neither is evidenced as an independent S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actionsconflict, planexecutionbatches, child scope gate and parent task result transport. - Closure path: tool calls and child outputs return to the main work loop, but no S2-specific disturbance→coordination→changed distinct-S1 behavior cycle was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting generic child tasks and preventing same-file parallel writes do not, without a concrete inter-S1 disturbance relation, establish a distinct VSM S2 function. - Evidence: src/kite/agent/parallel.py, src/kite/agent/orchestrator.py, src/kite/agent/loop.py. - Basis: bounded source inspection and explicit counterfactual. - Confidence: moderate; narrowly scoped to the shipped session architecture and inspected conflict-handling paths. - Caveats: a custom deployment could compose children into organizational S1s, but that external composition is not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-session current-regulation function with its own discretionary system-wide decision and return is established beyond user task governance and local action management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budgets, mode/permission constraints, tool and child capacity, and task completion are current operational constraints, not automatically S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: substantive current allocation/priority/commitment intervention across the system would be needed; no such first-party S3-specific rights path was located. - Decision owner: user selects task and approval/mode; the running model selects work actions; deterministic runtime enforces limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run budgets, interrupt, user steering, mode restrictions, policy engine, child spawn limits and session state. - Closure path: budget/permission gates can stop or alter a tool action; no independent S3-level current-regulatory decision and subsequent S1 return distinct from ordinary execution is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: session-level planning, workflow stages, human tool approval and agent self-steering are not sufficient for whole-system current control. - Evidence: src/kite/agent/loop.py, src/kite/agent/runtime.py, src/kite/agent/mode.py, src/kite/application/policy.py. - Basis: source-first negative determination. - Confidence: moderate for selected recursion and standard distribution. - Caveats: operator-guided plan/build progression exists, but no separate legitimate S3 parent-mode closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: plausible but unestablished function-specific complementary checking of coding outcome claims, rather than ordinary in-loop test evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an agent could claim successful edits or tests when required checks did not pass; verification collector and submission gate attenuate some of that risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verification collector may block submission and request check evidence; whether this is an independently owned complementary audit of S1 performance rather than ordinary task-execution validation is unresolved. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party collector/check planning; the main agent executes check commands and receives their tool outcomes. No independent auditor/claim-adjudication owner is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EvidenceVerifier.consume derives records from tool results, submitblockreason and VerificationCollector, plus user-visible status and tool-result nudges. - Closure path: command result → verification records/status → potentially blocked submit or requested additional checks → normal agent work continues; no demonstrated separate independently owned audit-judgment/organizational return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main coding actor can select and run checks; verification derives evidence independently of model-written claims but a separate complementary audit function/owner is not demonstrated solely by parsing the same check stream. - Evidence: src/kite/agent/verification.py, src/kite/application/verification.py, src/kite/agent/loop.py, src/kite/data/subagents/reviewer.md. - Basis: explicit positive checking implementation, insufficient evidence for S3 ownership/independence threshold. - Confidence: high for source mechanics, unresolved classification of whether the path forms a genuinely complementary audit. - Caveats: shipped reviewer persona is task text, not evidence of an independently executed reviewer, independent test process or recognized audit mandate. The narrow ? is not a claim that checks are absent.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no established outside-environment intelligence and prospective capability-adaptation loop at this session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: context loss and changing task details; session compaction and memory handle continuity, not demonstrably future organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a prospective change to the harness capability or future operating strategy based on external/future signals, then return that choice into current capability; not evidenced. - Decision owner: model may revise a current task plan while external providers and users supply prompts; local memory and compaction are deterministic/summarizing support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory, checkpoint/handoff, preserved context, goal/session persistence and context compaction. - Closure path: saved context can influence subsequent turns, but no established outside-and-then signal → adaptation option → implemented capability change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory and generic re-planning inside a coding task do not demonstrate an S4 function with a prospective/environmental remit. - Evidence: src/kite/memory/session.py, src/kite/agent/compaction.py, src/kite/memory/goal.py, src/kite/memory/contextcheckpoint.py. - Basis: inspected shipped memory/continuity modes. - Confidence: moderate; no uninspected custom plugins credited. - Caveats: user-installed skills/plugins might supply adaptations, but external future constructors are outside this pinned first-party ownership claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party organizational identity/ultimate-policy decision function with legitimate enclosing authority and returned policy closure is established at the session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: policy violations and unsafe tool requests are constrained, but tool-approval and configuration limits alone are not evidence of S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: set or reinterpret the identity and ultimate policy of the whole system and cause a returned decision to govern current operation; absent as a distinct first-party function. - Decision owner: the human operator configures settings, authorizes tools and supplies the task; installed runtime enforces policies. No evidence that the user performs a specified first-party S5 identity/ultimate-policy function rather than ordinary local operation control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval modes, project trust guardrails, system prompts, risk policy, config files and cancellation. - Closure path: permissions can allow/deny local actions, but there is no evidenced S5-grade identity/policy deliberation and return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policies constrain a coding agent; neither model prompt nor external operator approval by itself establishes ultimate organizational authority. - Evidence: src/kite/agent/mode.py, src/kite/application/policy.py, src/kite/guardrails/projecttrust.py, src/kite/config/user.py. - Basis: bounded first-party policy/authority inspection. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: a higher-level organization can govern its installed Kite workers, but that external parent&#39;s S5 is not this repository&#39;s credited function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/beaf0cf484ce7913efb9e58792849c70e537a6cf/assessments/kite.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit beaf0cf484ce7913efb9e58792849c70e537a6cf\">beaf0cf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T01:21:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shaneburrell/quikagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">QuikAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shaneburrell/quikagent/tree/d5851f84de2354f42b555367efaa339feaa46765\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d5851f84de2354f42b555367efaa339feaa46765\">d5851f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Open-ended model-guided editing, investigation and tool-driven coding in a configured QuikAgent workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requirements, changing file states, command errors, tool outputs and model-selected next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses task-relevant tools and iteratively responds to returned results or emits a final answer. - Decision owner: QuikAgent main model-guided Agent.Run; the runtime executes choices, and the operator governs privileged effects. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenAI-compatible model client, tools registry, router when enabled, tool approval, JSONL session, context compaction and optional subagents. - Closure path: User request → streamed model output/tool call → runToolBatch permission-checked tool execution → messages with observed results → next model turn or final response; session retained. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI TUI/print and loopback web paths instantiate the agent in normal operation; Agent.Run and bundled tools are first-party installed, not an external custom harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model-selected next-tool policy leaves only a deterministic tool/UI host; configured human approval limits effects but does not own every coding choice. - Evidence: cmd/quikagent/main.go; internal/agent/agent.go; internal/tools/tool.go; internal/llm/client.go; internal/session/session.go - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Deterministic prevention of shared-file write interference between distinct concurrently eligible model-guided plan-step workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently executing coding child S1 operations can target overlapping file sets in a plan wave; running both simultaneously would contend for the same files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select non-overlapping eligible steps for concurrent dispatch, defer overlapping steps and release them in later waves after earlier work reaches a terminal wave status. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic plan-wave orchestrator and file-overlap selector; no independent model-owned coordination policy assumed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PlanStep.Files and Deps, maxParallelWorkers=3, pathOverlap/filesOverlap, scoped child edit tools, wave waitgroup, reviewer and step status tracking. - Closure path: Confirmed plan handoff → nextWave selects eligible pending steps → pickNonOverlapping excludes conflicting file regions → concurrently launch non-overlapping subagents → wait and record done/failed states → subsequent nextWave admits formerly blocked steps into later execution. Supported test covers same-file serial execution. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Agent.Run dispatches runOrchestrated on supported plan &#39;go&#39; handoff, with model not pinned, and child Agent executes the planned work with first-party tools; this is an installed selectable mode, not docs/design future jobs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actual conflict-sensitive decision is a compiled deterministic relation in the shipped runtime, not a model autonomously adjudicating competition; S2=C is deliberately limited to this source-defined scheduler mode. - Evidence: internal/agent/orchestrate.go; internal/agent/orchestratetest.go; internal/agent/subagent.go; internal/tools/plan.go; internal/agent/agent.go - Basis: explicit, structural and unit-test fixture -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No established whole-QuikAgent current-control function owning shared organization commitments/priorities/resources beyond a user task plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workspace capacity, changing steps, user approvals, model utilization and task statuses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Would require a manager&#39;s whole-system current view and authoritative reallocation of operational priorities or commitments across S1 work. - Decision owner: No qualifying distinct owner; user approves a plan and deterministic orchestrator enforces wave constraints for the current coding task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent plan/todo state, model router, plan wave dispatch, session state, step limits and web/TUI reporting. - Closure path: Plan changes and child results flow back into one requested task and its step execution; no standalone whole-current management decision governing a portfolio of independent ongoing operations. - Boundary reachability: No S3 positive mode evidenced in installed CLI, agent, server and plan infrastructure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A task plan, approval button or runtime concurrency gate cannot itself decide whole-system commitments and restore cross-S1 current control. - Evidence: internal/agent/orchestrate.go; internal/tools/plan.go; internal/agent/agent.go; internal/session/plan.go; internal/server/server.go - Basis: bounded first-party negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent audit of a whole-system S3 management account with authoritative corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Erroneous worker completion reports, code quality defects, misleading test outcomes or audit omissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Would require complementary evidence access, independent judgment over a managerial claim, and feedback to accountable whole-current control. - Decision owner: The optional child reviewer uses a model to check step outputs, but is task-work QA serving the same orchestrator, not an S3 auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewWave, smallmodel selection, PASS/FAIL parser, event tracing, feedback to failed/done step statuses and optional user approvals. - Closure path: A code reviewer may mark a current plan wave as failed, not inspect and correct independent manager claims through a separate complementary regulatory channel. - Boundary reachability: The reviewWave source is reachable within installed plan orchestration but cannot be promoted to S3 solely from a model label or separate child context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer isolation and ordinary task-acceptance decisions are not enough to establish independent S3 report challenge and corrective governance. - Evidence: internal/agent/orchestrate.go; internal/agent/orchestratetest.go; internal/agent/subagent.go; docs/architecture.md - Basis: bounded first-party negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No observed first-party future viability intelligence that selects and installs durable new agent capability or strategy from prospective external assessment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing tools/providers, technology environment, project requirements, new model choices and future workflow needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A qualified S4 actor would generate prospective adaptation options and decide/reinstate adopted capability in later operation. - Decision owner: Not established; user chooses model, config, skills/agents and developers evolve repo; optional router routes current tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model router, persisted plans/JSONL sessions, custom agents/skills, configurable hooks and roadmap design documents. - Closure path: Current routing, session reuse and plan editing affect immediate task execution, not independently evidenced external-and-future analysis → adaptation choice → durable adopted next-generation capability. - Boundary reachability: No S4 positive in shipped runtime; proposed daemon/jobs/projects design docs are not credited as executed capabilities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Session memory and selecting a current model are task-level support; developer roadmap and external plugin possibilities cannot donate first-party S4. - Evidence: internal/router/router.go; internal/session/session.go; internal/agent/agent.go; docs/extending.md; docs/design/README.md - Basis: bounded first-party negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No shipped legitimate ultimate purpose/identity decision loop returning an authoritative decision to the agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Requests beyond configured bounds, unsafe shell/file operations, operator model choices and external service credentials. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A qualified S5 owner would decide legitimate system purpose and ultimate policy then send an enforceable return to operation. - Decision owner: Operator supplies instructions, keys and tool approval, but no source-installed organization-level function-specific parent S5 is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RuleMatcher, CheckPermission, TUI/web approval, sandbox tools, setup/YAML and user docs. - Closure path: Permission checks filter individual tool requests; the returned allow/deny is an execution gate, not a full identity/ultimate-policy issue processed and decided with governance feedback. - Boundary reachability: No positive S5 mode credited; tool policies are shipped but are distinct from a complete S5 owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static allow/deny rules and user task directives enforce boundaries chosen elsewhere rather than constitute first-party ultimate policy authority. - Evidence: internal/tools/permissions.go; internal/tools/tool.go; internal/config/config.go; cmd/quikagent/main.go; docs/config.md - Basis: bounded first-party negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/198117d482391be10a964309a2559c766971b1f1/assessments/qu/quikagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 198117d482391be10a964309a2559c766971b1f1\">198117d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T15:24:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aw1875/synth\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">synth</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aw1875/synth/tree/545f1c2dbef396a2a693fd3a6c7f11ea4d00ce19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 545f1c2dbef396a2a693fd3a6c7f11ea4d00ce19\">545f1c2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify, execute and verify software in response to a coding objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository state, implementation choices, build/test/tool failures, provider/model variation, context pressure, permission decisions and focused research questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret returned evidence, decide when read-only subagent research is useful and decide when the requested work is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed synth agent in the selected Build/Plan/Review mode; Build mode owns project-changing software-engineering outcomes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in file/bash/web/MCP/skill/todo tools, approval gate, safety classifier, hooks, provider adapters, steering, persistence, compaction, cancellation and turn limits. - Closure path: user request → model selects coding/tool/research action → first-party runtime executes or delegates a read-only task → evidence returns into the same parent loop → model revises or completes the objective. - Boundary reachability: the shipped TUI and synth run headless path instantiate the same AgentLoop/tool/provider machinery; Build is the default selectable agent mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes, constrains and persists work, but the model makes the open-ended engineering choices and interprets the evidence. - Evidence: README.md; src/agent/loop.zig; src/agent/agent.zig; src/agent/prompt.zig. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Build-mode write/command calls may require human approval; approval constrains execution rather than supplying the substantive coding decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete interference among distinct concurrently project-mutating S1 units is placed under a dedicated attenuation/feedback relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task subagents have independent nested loops and tool calls may execute concurrently, but the built-in task-agent capability is read-only and cannot create file-write conflicts with the parent or peers. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrency and nested agents are present, but the shipped multi-agent path deliberately removes project mutation from children; no function-specific peer-interference control loop is needed or instantiated. - Evidence: src/agent/agent.zig; src/agent/subagent.zig; src/tools/task.zig. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shared provider/backend and tool scheduling are execution plumbing, not S2 without a concrete distinct-S1 interference witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established above the active coding loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no portfolio of independently controlled current S1 commitments/resources is exposed to a separate actor with substantive allocation/intervention authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mid-turn steering, cancel/timeout/token/repeat limits, live read-only subagent transcript viewing and parent-turn cancellation propagation alter or terminate one current S1 turn but do not create a whole-system controller. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model may choose to call task, but each task call waits for a bounded read-only child result; there is no model-owned live worker portfolio with selective reassignment/retry/resource control. User steering is injected into the same parent loop at its next model step. - Evidence: src/agent/loop.zig; src/agent/subagent.zig; src/tui/model.zig; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic stop/cancel, mode switching, todo state and direct steering of one operational loop do not satisfy the Methodology 0.3.6 whole-current threshold.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no first-party path automatically assigns a coding claim to an independently instantiated reviewer and returns an audit judgment into corrective operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review mode provides read/run-only tools and explicit review instructions, and generic task subagents can gather evidence, but these are user-selected modes/tools inside the same session rather than an independent review constructor. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: switching the same session/model loop from Build to Review changes its capability/prompt but does not create an independent author-reviewer boundary; task subagents are generic read-only researchers, not a dedicated audit verdict path. - Evidence: src/agent/agent.zig; src/agent/loop.zig; src/agent/subagent.zig; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a person can manually use Review mode after a Build turn, but generic human sequencing of the same loop is not a standard independent S3 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no changing external/future condition is transformed by an adaptation owner into options that modify later organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent sessions/todos, transcript search, context compaction, skills, web tools, provider/model switching, MCP and user-configured lifecycle hooks supply information or configuration but do not close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retained sessions and skills can inform later work, and hooks can deterministically block/transform operations, but no first-party actor is shown developing prospective adaptation options from outside/future evidence and returning them into current capability. - Evidence: README.md; src/core/conversation.zig; src/agent/context.zig; src/core/hooks.zig. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external web/MCP content and persisted context are evidence/capability inputs, not S4 ownership by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is routed to an authoritative owner and returned as governing runtime policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approvals, safe-command rules, user-configured blocking hooks, agent-mode tool allowlists, system prompt replacement and provider/MCP configuration constrain operation but do not establish S5. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coding agent follows developer/user-authored policy and cannot authoritatively redefine synth&#39;s identity or ultimate operating principles. - Evidence: src/agent/safety.zig; src/agent/agent.zig; src/core/hooks.zig; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a blocking hook may encode a strong local policy, but the hook is externally configured deterministic enforcement rather than a first-party identity-governance owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/03b00ab1eb2a523f621da24dd5d19128b2703b77/assessments/synth.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 03b00ab1eb2a523f621da24dd5d19128b2703b77\">03b00ab &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T19:24:04+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/speakeasy-api/kit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/speakeasy-api/kit/tree/3fd1fd5fda269569ceba0c2dd884c988d31f53d0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3fd1fd5fda269569ceba0c2dd884c988d31f53d0\">3fd1fd5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously carry out coding tasks with model-selected Compose programs and observation-driven follow-up. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repo discovery, edited files, tests, command failures, subprocess outputs and varying subtask context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select Runlet compose program, branches, tool operations, edits and subsequent steps according to observed results. - Decision owner: model-directed installed Kit coding actor; imported AgentKit loop library supplies a generic executor while Kit supplies the shipped local Compose programming/tool/session runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime.runpersistentinner, Agent::builder with Compose source, shell/read/edit tools, tool-run observations, durable session and user-selected model. - Closure path: user task→Kit-created model session→model emits Compose program→Runlet and first-party local tools execute→returned output reaches model→next program or final answer. - Boundary reachability: shipped TUI, prompt, ACP and A2A modes all use Kit&#39;s session/runtime and local tool registry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive next task operations are chosen by the model rather than fixed orchestration; source-specific program tool registry and observed feedback are integrated into an installed product. - Evidence: src/runtime.rs, src/tools/mod.rs, src/tools/shell.rs, src/tools/edit.rs, README.md. - Basis: source-backed installed agent/tool feedback, dependency-labeled. - Confidence: high for first-party wired path; task performance not tested. - Caveats: imported agentkit-loop implements generic model loop, and external ACP agent behaviors are excluded.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party interference-specific feedback loop between separate first-party operational S1 divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel Compose operations could touch overlapping files; two requests to the same reusable child can race its session generation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate S1 units mutually rescope, serialize conflicting work or receive a conflict-specific correction from a coordinator; not established. - Decision owner: Compose ordering is model-authored execution programming; child registry enforces one child session&#39;s handle/generation validity; neither constitutes a distinct S2 work division coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel Runlet calls, after dependency edges, per-child mutex, monotonically checked session generations, stale-handle refusal and resource limits. - Closure path: stale child-generation request fails rather than corrupting that one session; no observed cross-worker interference detector/relation returns to alter distinct operational S1 commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic concurrency control in one child object&#39;s lifecycle, explicit programming dependencies and task fan-out are insufficient for autonomous or constructor S2 over distinct S1 divisions. - Evidence: src/tools/subagent.rs, src/runtime.rs, README.md, src/session/children.rs. - Basis: bounded negative with explicitly considered generation lock. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: external user-written concurrent programs could construct coordination, and independently imported ACP agents may have their own controllers, but neither is credited to Kit&#39;s shipped organizational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current control decision owner separate from the coding agent&#39;s task loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: step budget, model failure, user interruption, child depth/capacity, pending background jobs and resume state are ordinary execution disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select whole-current organization of multiple S1 units and enforce modified operational commitments from an independent current controller; not found. - Decision owner: user controls one session, model authors Compose programs, runtime limits/retries sessions and child concurrency; no separate privileged whole-current judgment/feedback organ. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent transcript and locks, cancellation, child generation/caps, TUI controls and request budgets. - Closure path: local run cancellation or child stale-handle rejection changes a task; no whole-current multi-unit supervisory regulation, state inspection and returned new system-wide commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Kit does not claim full organization of all connected third-party ACP agents; child task delegation, scripting and deterministic safety gates are not a distinct S3 current-control actor. - Evidence: src/runtime.rs, src/tools/subagent.rs, src/session.rs, src/requestbudget.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: an external operator could organize many Kit instances, but that is outside this single-session assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently assigned first-party claim-audit owner or binding findings-to-repair loop in the installed local coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false code-correctness/completion claims; ordinary model may run tests, and optional eval questions may inspect a state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent review of producer code/claims with auditable findings and subsequent controller/repair disposition; no qualifying path shown. - Decision owner: model using ordinary shell, externally supplied Jev eval questions when enabled, or externally configured ACP reviewer; none is a first-party dedicated complementary auditor for the selected task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Compose test calls, external EvalTool, child prompt output schema and session history. - Closure path: any eval/child result returns as a normal model tool observation with no claim-specific mandatory review gate or guaranteed accountable correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user/model-callable evaluator and general child role prompt are not an automatically assigned evidence-taking complementary audit owner. - Evidence: src/tools/eval.rs, src/runtime.rs, src/tools/subagent.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high for the default first-party session. - Caveats: external evaluation can still be useful to the user, but not automatically equivalent to first-party independent S3 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separately owned external/prospective capability adaptation to future tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: long context, changing model preferences and persistent task history are handled by compaction/config, not a distinct future-environment intelligence remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: analyze outside/long-term change, select new capabilities and return them to future operation in an owned feedback cycle; absent. - Decision owner: operator changes provider/model/config/plugins; installed coding agent has no source-proven strategic future capability owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable JSONL transcript, compaction, plugin load, model selection and Kit binary update. - Closure path: resuming/replaying current session or installing third-party plugin does not itself establish prospective adaptation strategy with selected return to S1/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: saving memory and allowing extension installation are capability primitives, not an observed first-party external-intelligence organization. - Evidence: src/compaction.rs, src/plugins.rs, src/update.rs, src/session.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: user-created higher-level workflows could adapt Kit externally, not assessed here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct rightful organizational identity/ultimate-policy controller at this coding-session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/provider security requirements, tool scopes and child headless permission prompts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate ultimate system-purpose policy adjudication with authoritative return into system operation; not proven. - Decision owner: external user/configured security container owns task and permissions. Kit explicitly states it is not a security boundary and does not ship a permissions framework. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: root directory choice, subagent limits, headless permission refusal and external sandbox. - Closure path: a headless child permission prompt is rejected/failed, or environment provides a sandbox, but no whole-identity S5 decision/return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: rejecting risky child permissions, external environment policy and user model selection are ordinary operating constraints, not S5 legitimacy/identity authority. - Evidence: README.md, src/tools/subagent.rs, src/runtime.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: high that no distinct first-party S5 is described at this boundary. - Caveats: an adopting external organization may govern the binary and tools as its own S5; no such parent rights are invented here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d32058d7d6bc6242b20ec5d3a58150eb7ac8a2c0/assessments/kit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d32058d7d6bc6242b20ec5d3a58150eb7ac8a2c0\">d32058d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T17:06:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/fbrubacher/orch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">orch</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/fbrubacher/orch/tree/16960b40ed6293e27e4536bbd99d62989264a87e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 16960b40ed6293e27e4536bbd99d62989264a87e\">16960b4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed bounded coding work on a caller-selected repository: investigate, plan, modify code, validate, receive reviewer feedback and produce an answer/optional PR. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown bug/feature, variable repository structure, tool failures, failed validation, inconsistent plan and reviewer rejection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Router may classify task type; Planner chooses exploratory tool calls and proposes plan; Executor chooses first-party read/write/shell/git tools, then Reviewer examines reports/diff/test output and judges PASS/FIX. The Orchestrator decides phase transitions and iteration budget deterministically. - Decision owner: Configured first-party model-backed Agent instances own case-specific task/tool selection and model-based reviewer judgment; the Python orchestrator owns deterministic workflow sequencing and retry count. Human selects task/repo/credentials and controls external merge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared Agent.run model/tool loop and dispatch, tool registry and writable/read-only tool splits, OpenRouter client, typed Router/Planner/Reviewer outputs, WorkflowState persistence, optional finalizer and git PR skills. - Closure path: CLI/library request → Router model or default workflow → Planner model + read tools → Executor model + write/shell/git tools → validation/diff → Reviewer model PASS/FIX → on FIX next executor consumes reviewer feedback → final result/checkpoint; optional tool-using finalizer opens PR when local gh and configured target permit. - Boundary reachability: Normal shipped Orchestrator.run instantiates and calls the role classes wired through Agent.run, which executes each selected tool and returns its observation to the subsequent model turn; main.py invokes this path, not only a demo/test placeholder. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model inference leaves a deterministic phase shell without selecting task-specific investigation/edit/review judgments; using an external inference API doesn&#39;t transfer the first-party agent/workflow implementation to OpenRouter. - Evidence: main.py; orchestrator/orchestrator.py; agents/base.py; models/openrouter.py; tools/registry.py; agents/router.py; agents/planner.py; agents/executor.py; agents/reviewer.py; tools/factory.py; tools/workspace.py; tools/shell.py; tools/filesystem.py; tools/git.py - Basis: structural (shipped first-party executable source, not live paid model performance) - Confidence: High for reachable configured first-party S1; runtime quality untested. - Caveats: Credentials/provider required for actual model run; source alone does not demonstrate successful external PR or useful coding outcome. The external target project remains outside the assessed local harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced continuing inter-S1 mutual-regulation function at an organizational recursion above independently viable operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plan/execution mismatch and reviewer disagreement within a single finite coding task, potential read/write overlap between role phases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Orchestrator.run serially calls distinct role instances and passes plans/review feedback; its fixed phase machine attenuates intra-task mismatch but does not negotiate disruptive commitments among independently viable continuing S1 businesses. - Decision owner: Deterministic phase machine plus role-specific model decisions in one task, rather than an independently mandated inter-unit S2 regulator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Router/Planner/Executor/Reviewer sequence, state.history, checkpoint data, event emissions and read-only vs writable tool access. - Closure path: Task planning → execution → reviewer verdict → same task&#39;s corrective retry; no cross-business operational interference → independent S2 balancing decision → two viable S1s adapt their continuing behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple sequential agent roles are parts of a single job pipeline, not continuing independent viable S1 departments. A review feedback loop is not automatically an S2 relationship. - Evidence: main.py; orchestrator/orchestrator.py; agents/router.py; agents/planner.py; agents/executor.py; agents/reviewer.py; orchestrator/state.py; orchestrator/persistence.py - Basis: bounded first-party absence - Confidence: High at declared single coding-workflow recursion. - Caveats: A higher-recursion organization could run many orch jobs, but that organization is not packaged in this standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No established accountable current whole-organization management of continuing viable operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task delays, failing validation, wrong workflow, retries, incomplete edits and need for a protected merge decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configurable model routing, max iterations, deterministic workflow and Reviewer.passed determine local task sequencing; human may choose a repository/skill and later decide merge. No discretionary whole-system resource/commitment allocation across multiple viable S1 units is wired. - Decision owner: Caller config and deterministic orchestrator for task execution, model reviewer for task correctness; no present S3 executive or reconstructable parent S3 mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OrchestratorConfig, workflow table, retry budget, state/persistence, validation steps, task status/output and optional PR finalizer. - Closure path: One coding request → quality check → repeat/finalize task; no continuing operations portfolio reporting → current management allocation or intervention → binding updated organizational commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Calling the top-level class Orchestrator does not make it a VSM S3 current manager. An operator choosing task or approving a PR is not a packaged parent-governed organizational S3 loop. - Evidence: main.py; orchestrator/orchestrator.py; orchestrator/config.py; skills/README.md; skills/open-pr.md; orchestrator/state.py; orchestrator/persistence.py; agents/router.py; agents/planner.py; agents/executor.py; agents/reviewer.py - Basis: bounded first-party absence - Confidence: High at declared single-task pipeline recursion. - Caveats: Repo ownership and human downstream merge decisions are external to this harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent field-reality audit challenging ordinary S3 management accounts and returning an S3 corrective decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Executor may report incorrect success or validation; diff and tests may disagree with its narrative. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer agent inspects executor summary, actual git diff and test/validation text and returns PASS/FIX; this is a substantive task-level quality check but not auditing managerial reality at an S3 recursion. - Decision owner: Model-backed ReviewerAgent does the coding-work artifact check; no chartered complementary auditor of an existing current organizational manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: gitdiff, runvalidation, read-only ReviewerAgent, PASS/FIX loop and events. - Closure path: Executor output + diff/test observations → reviewer judgment → next coding attempt or result; no S3 normal management report → materially separate operations channel → management intervention. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Code reviewer is not automatically S3; source shows different role and evidence but its object is the same bounded S1 product, not the veracity of S3 management statements about a multi-unit organization. - Evidence: main.py; orchestrator/orchestrator.py; agents/router.py; agents/planner.py; agents/executor.py; agents/reviewer.py; tools/factory.py; tools/workspace.py; tools/shell.py; tools/filesystem.py; tools/git.py - Basis: bounded absence after inspecting strongest audit-like path - Confidence: Medium-high: reviewer independence is real at code-artifact level; no S3 reporting/audit role established. - Caveats: A different declared higher-level organization could make separate use of these checks; not evidenced here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No continuing future-environment intelligence selecting prospective organizational capability changes and returning accepted adaptations to present operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New task categories, unfamiliar project architecture, evolving model choices, reusable skills and workflow variations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Router chooses a predefined workflow for the current task; OrchestratorConfig and skill files are maintainer/operator-set. The code does not autonomously investigate future organizational opportunities, select and accept new capability strategy. - Decision owner: Human developer/config and task-specific router model; no organizational S4 adaptation decision right or parent-governed strategic return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Router classifications, configurable model overrides, prompts, prewritten skills and workflow table. - Closure path: Present task → task-class workflow selection → coding execution; no outside-environment search → strategic capability option → legitimate acceptance → revised organization-wide capability and current S3 operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model selection for today&#39;s task and future extensibility described in README are not an implemented recurring S4 prospective intelligence function. - Evidence: main.py; orchestrator/orchestrator.py; orchestrator/config.py; skills/README.md; skills/open-pr.md; agents/router.py; agents/planner.py; agents/executor.py; agents/reviewer.py; README.md - Basis: bounded first-party absence - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Developer may extend prompts/workflows in later revisions, which is outside pinned source.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate purpose/identity and constitutional policy authority at the declared organizational recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe shell edits, scope violation, model choice, skill authority and external PR ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Target workspace paths are sandboxed, read/write tools are role-gated, configuration defines fixed budgets/default workflows, and a caller controls credentials/merge. This enforces limits but cannot decide and return a constitutional organization-identity issue. - Decision owner: External user/developer controls boundaries; deterministic tool registry/workspace enforce them. No first-party policy/identity deliberator or packaged parent S5 return path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace and filesystem checks, read-only tooling for reviewers, configurable task skills, default prompt, no automatic PR merge. - Closure path: Operator sets configuration/target and permissions → tool and workflow execution within limits; no organization-identity issue → legitimate S5 authority → binding constitutional decision returned into ongoing organizational operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool sandbox and human permission are necessary safety mechanisms but not S5. The human owns the project externally, with no first-party organization-level policy judgment protocol in orch. - Evidence: tools/factory.py; tools/workspace.py; tools/shell.py; tools/filesystem.py; tools/git.py; orchestrator/config.py; skills/README.md; skills/open-pr.md; README.md - Basis: bounded first-party absence - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Upstream governance of the OSS source itself is adjacent to the published runtime and not assumed to operate a VSM S5 for the target user&#39;s job.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8ff8aabc9dfcea22ed25a67f311e076e7dbba01b/assessments/or/orch.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8ff8aabc9dfcea22ed25a67f311e076e7dbba01b\">8ff8aab &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-21T23:34:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FacileStudio/kori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kori</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-21\">2026-10-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FacileStudio/kori/tree/960e7cb242a64f271afefbe194cf10926db00eca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 960e7cb242a64f271afefbe194cf10926db00eca\">960e7cb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform open-ended, model-directed coding/investigation tasks through a first-party installed coding agent using file, shell (where enabled), web, MCP and project-context tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended task requirements, evolving repository state, tool errors, tests, diagnosis and changing user instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The running model-agent chooses next tool actions, observes their outcomes and revises execution until an answer or stop condition; operator may gate individual effects. - Decision owner: Model-directed Kori-assembled Nacelle Agent operating through shipped supported Kori tool and user-session paths; Nacelle owns generic loop implementation, not Kori&#39;s local configured task identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party assemble tool wiring, provider selection, Nacelle model/tool API, TUI/headless dispatcher, permissions, sessions, task tool, compaction, optional diagnostics and user hooks. - Closure path: User coding request → Kori provider/tool/approval composition → Nacelle-backed agent proposes tool action → Kori-visible tool output/event → model observes output and acts or returns answer → Kori records/presents result. - Boundary reachability: The shipped builder executes nacelle.New(cfg), while TUI runner and headless runner expose the actual session. This is a product-wired agent path, not merely SDK example code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: With the configured model decision process removed, the Kori UI and registered tools do not choose open-ended source modifications or follow-up exploration. Kori has a working agent session through an external SDK, but the agent&#39;s task-semantic decisions occur in that instantiated first-party configuration. - Evidence: builder; TUI; CLI; README. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: high for shipped executable assembly; no live model/provider effectiveness inferred - Caveats: Nacelle is a genuine loop dependency; this assessment does not claim Kori independently implements the underlying generic engine. Shell availability and approvals depend on configuration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party path was established that specifically attenuates actual or structurally demonstrated interference/oscillation between distinct S1 coding work cells, beyond generic asynchronous delegation/result routing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent child-agent work and possible collision on shared editor state, files, approvals and agent messaging. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A qualifying S2 would detect/prevent an inter-S1 interference relation and return its resolution to affected sibling cells. Kori code principally launches independent subtasks and reassembles their outputs; it does not own a shown cross-child conflict or deconfliction judgment. - Decision owner: No distinct first-party S2 owner identified. Nacelle owns the delegated agents&#39; concurrency bounds; Kori&#39;s TUI controls detached output/display and the parent&#39;s subsequent synthesis. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: withParallelAgents, concurrent Nacelle DelegateParallel, per-batch/index result channel, UI serialized update loop, user-message queue, optional cancellation and display spend accounting. - Closure path: Child results return to the parent display or synthesis, but that is generic result aggregation. A supported sibling-contention→attenuation→changed competing S1 execution path was not established. - Boundary reachability: No positive S2 claimed. The opt-in first-party delegation tool and TUI result handler are accessible, but coordinate reports and UI state, not an evidenced shared work conflict. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scheduling independent jobs and rendering asynchronous messages are not function-specific S2 evidence without a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. User messages queued during a busy parent turn are a parent-input ordering concern, not interference between two autonomous task agents. - Evidence: parallel assembly; fan-out; result handler; delivery test. - Basis: structural bounded negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No independently established whole-Kori current-control manager over multiple S1 units&#39; collective resources, priorities, commitments or corrective tradeoffs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple unfinished subtasks, diagnostic failures, scheduled sessions, tool approvals and resource expenditure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose current cross-work priorities or allocation based on a whole-organization situation view and return the choice into constituent S1 work. - Decision owner: No demonstrated global executive agent or legitimate parent-regulated whole-system S3. Operators steer individual runs and configure caps; Kori presents updates and task-level controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TUI multiagent display, task plan, max-iterations, parallel concurrency caps, cron records, diagnostic gates, interrupt/cancel and operator session settings. - Closure path: Session-local plan and task progress update the current task, not a sourced whole-current organizational resource/commitment decision feeding multiple independent S1 cells. - Boundary reachability: No positive path credited; configured parallel assembly and cron run records are included in inspected scope. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model task planning, human steering and deterministic guards do not create a whole-system managerial owner. - Evidence: task-run assembly; parallel implementation; cron log; README. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent complement of normal S1/management reporting with a distinct audit finding and corrective whole-system return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False claims of coding success, failing tool/test runs and misleading session outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare ordinary reporting with independent direct evidence and return corrective finding to current management. - Decision owner: No distinct independent complementary auditor established in the shipped Kori installation. User-specified tests and optional diagnostics run in ordinary task workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Post-edit diagnostic gate, source/tool results, session transcript, configurable hook trust, project tests and IDE event surfaces. - Closure path: Diagnostic checks can produce findings or refuse an edit in the task, but a separate independent audit mandate/access and corrective management return is not evidenced. - Boundary reachability: No positive audit path; configured diagnostics are a shipped per-edit gate rather than whole-organization independent S3 audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A deterministic post-edit check, a reviewer skill that users could install or a distinct background worker does not by itself give independent complementary audit authority. - Evidence: diagnostic gate; builder; parallel result. - Basis: structural bounded negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidence of a shipped prospective outside-environment intelligence/adaptation loop that selects future capability changes and returns them into current Kori operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving tool/model availability, recurring incidents, newly required tasks and future operating constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select a forward-looking adaptation option on new environmental evidence, approve/adopt it and alter future installed agent capability or organization. - Decision owner: Human chooses provider/settings/tools and may write plugins/skills. Runtime compaction and diagnostic checks act on existing conversation/tool state rather than a future-directed capability adaptation choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Config, provider resolver, skill discovery, short-term context compaction, optional external compaction judge and cron scheduling/logging. - Closure path: Kori reacts to present task results and user configuration. No first-party prospective selection→adoption→later capability modification closure or parent-governed S4 mode is established. - Boundary reachability: No positive path; shipped compaction policy and scheduled-run state do not demonstrate S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Saving context and selecting provider under user configuration solve execution context, not future organizational intelligence. - Evidence: policy; settings; cron. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy governance body with a decisive policy revision right and return to Kori agent operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts between agent activity, user goals and long-lived ultimate operating rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide which ultimate policy/identity may govern Kori&#39;s organization and return its adopted outcome into future agent work. - Decision owner: User/developer configures model, source root, approval policy, tools and skills; no first-party agent or separately constituted legitimate parent S5 owner/path is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool approvals, hook trust, environment/path restrictions, settings parsing, CLI flags and task-system prompts. - Closure path: Operator config changes what a session may do; no explicit function-specific ultimate-policy decision and approved return-to-operation loop is installed. - Boundary reachability: No positive function claim; session-local approvals and settings exist but are not a first-party S5 decision owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Security checks and user approvals enforce tool-use constraints; an installed coding assistant does not automatically decide its own organizational identity or host ultimate purpose. - Evidence: README; builder; tool approvals. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a6094ce397eb9f8f6eddc7a14400ea00c76c7867/assessments/ko/kori.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a6094ce397eb9f8f6eddc7a14400ea00c76c7867\">a6094ce &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T02:40:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cargopete/gyrfalcon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gyrfalcon</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cargopete/gyrfalcon/tree/090615a2f13a062fd91896950106aeb505376c55\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 090615a2f13a062fd91896950106aeb505376c55\">090615a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform a Rust coding task by interpreting the user&#39;s request, gathering workspace/compiler evidence, choosing edits/tool actions and iterating on returned results until the agent ends the turn. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository structure, incomplete task information, type/compiler errors, failing commands, stale file state, refused tools, provider responses and local implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next repository/tool action, interpret its returned evidence, decide what code/content change to make and decide when the operational response is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Gyrfalcon agent in the provider session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolSet, exact/fingerprinted patching, approval policies, workspace fencing, OS sandboxing, bounded model turns, provider adapters, Cargo/process tools, session logging, resumable state and deterministic context elision. - Closure path: user request → model selects workspace/tool action → first-party runtime classifies/permits/executes it → result returns to the same provider session → model revises the plan or implementation → further actions/results continue until the model ends the turn. - Boundary reachability: both the standard interactive gyr path and gyr run call the same prepare path that constructs Agent with the standard tool set, provider session, approval policy and session sink, then invokes the agent loop directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime policy and sandbox code constrain what can happen, but removing the model removes the open-ended coding judgments; the deterministic layers do not independently select implementation edits or interpret task evidence. - Evidence: README.md; crates/gyr-core/src/lib.rs; crates/gyr-cli/src/main.rs; crates/gyr-eval/src/runner.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: mutating/process actions may require human approval in the default interactive mode, but the approval decision constrains execution rather than supplying the substantive coding decision; read-only and allow-all are alternate supported policy modes over the same agent loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function is established at the reviewed session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not applicable; the standard runtime does not construct multiple operational S1 units whose interactions require attenuation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential model turns, tool-call settlement, workspace fencing, approval and sandbox logic coordinate components of one S1 loop but do not coordinate distinct operational S1 units. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the architecture explicitly lists multi-agent orchestration as a non-goal, and the standard runtime constructs one Agent over one model session rather than peer operational agents. - Evidence: docs/rfcs/RFC-0001-architecture.md; crates/gyr-core/src/lib.rs; crates/gyr-cli/src/main.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider/tool/runtime components cooperate inside one coding loop; component sequencing is not promoted to S2 without distinct S1 units and a concrete inter-S1 disturbance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control function is established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no distinct whole-session resource/commitment/prioritization disturbance is placed under a metasystem decision right; current execution is bounded by static/deterministic limits and local operator controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-turn limit, approval policy, sandbox, Ctrl-C cancellation, /status, per-session model selection and deterministic context-budget thresholds constrain or expose the single S1 but do not constitute a separate inside-and-now control owner. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the core agent itself consumes tool evidence for its operational task, while deterministic limits and human stop/approval actions regulate local execution. No separate actor receives a whole-system current view and makes resource/commitment/prioritization decisions over the assessed organization. - Evidence: README.md; crates/gyr-cli/src/session.rs; crates/gyr-core/src/lib.rs; docs/rfcs/RFC-0013-context-budget.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator approval/cancellation and deterministic context elision are real control mechanisms, but Methodology 0.3.6 requires a whole-system current decision loop rather than generic operator UI, fixed budgets or termination enforcement.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit path is supplied in the standard runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no separate first-party channel independently challenges the coding agent&#39;s ordinary claims/results and returns findings into corrective operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the same agent can call Cargo/process tools and inspect compiler/test evidence; the withdrawn diagnostic gate can compute deterministic progress verdicts but is deliberately absent from the standard tool set; the eval corpus judges development/evaluation runs outside the assessed live session. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: compiler/tool feedback is available through the ordinary S1 observation path, not through an independent complementary auditor. The eval harness and repository CI are adjacent validation systems rather than standard-distribution runtime owners. - Evidence: README.md; crates/gyr-eval/src/runner.rs; docs/rfcs/RFC-0011-diagnostic-gate.md; evals/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: successful Cargo/tests can verify aspects of an implementation, but verification evidence used by the same ordinary agent does not by itself establish S3 independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective adaptation loop is established in the shipped runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external model/tool-performance changes and future capability choices are studied in repository eval/RFC work, but no live runtime actor owns their prospective interpretation and adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established inside the assessed runtime. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static model catalogue, user/project configuration, provider profiles, context-window metadata and the development eval corpus can inform a human or maintainer but do not autonomously close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coding agent operates against the model/tool/configuration already selected for the current session. Measured model/tool comparisons remain in adjacent eval/RFC artifacts and are not consumed by a first-party runtime adaptation actor that changes future capability. - Evidence: README.md; crates/gyr-cli/src/main.rs; crates/gyr-cli/src/config.rs; docs/rfcs/RFC-0012-eval-harness.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers can use eval findings to evolve the project and users can choose another model/configuration, but those external human development/configuration decisions are not a closed first-party S4 mode at the assessed session boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established at the reviewed session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no runtime identity or ultimate-policy issue is placed under an authoritative S5 decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: developer-authored system prompt, approval policy, sandbox rules, project/user configuration restrictions and tool schemas define operating constraints but do not provide a runtime owner that can legitimately establish or revise Gyrfalcon&#39;s identity/ultimate policy. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coding agent follows the shipped prompt and runtime policy; it does not own authority to redefine those governing principles. Human per-call approval and static configuration likewise do not form an identity/policy decision-return loop. - Evidence: crates/gyr-core/src/prompt.rs; README.md; crates/gyr-core/src/approval.rs; crates/gyr-cli/src/config.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository maintainers own project development policy outside the runtime boundary, and the operator owns local permission choices; neither is promoted to S5 without a first-party identity/ultimate-policy issue, authoritative decision and return-to-operation loop at the declared recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/edb9896409a0b83dd86d8112ecddb42997fc8973/assessments/gyrfalcon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit edb9896409a0b83dd86d8112ecddb42997fc8973\">edb9896 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T04:29:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dairycow/scout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Scout</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dairycow/scout/tree/1c7b51f4b03f719bedaf6a81dcdf896f6f39f806\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1c7b51f4b03f719bedaf6a81dcdf896f6f39f806\">1c7b51f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: achieve local coding/inspection goals by choosing tool actions and revising them after execution feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel user requests, unknown source structure, shell/file errors, available skill context and changed tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses registered tool names and arguments on successive calls, revising actions or terminating after observed outcomes. - Decision owner: the live provider-backed Agent model/tool conversation; the external user sets the task and plugin/provider context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registered tools and provider adapters, event bus, SQLite message projection, optional concurrent tool dispatch, config/skills prompting, tool-error return and turn bound. - Closure path: user prompt and session messages → model selects tool calls → registry executes first-party handlers → result/error events project into conversation → model observes changes and chooses another action or answer. - Boundary reachability: both interactive and -p paths construct the same Agent in host.boot; default built-in providers and native tools are registered by its shipped plugin manifest. The offline fake-client test directly exercises the tool-result feedback loop and error retries. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model choice is not predetermined by the CLI or registry; execution feedback changes subsequent model-selected actions, satisfying local adaptive operations. Executor concurrency does not create additional owners. - Evidence: scout/cli.py; scout/agent.py; scout/host.py; tests/testagent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural + deterministic fake-client script tests. - Confidence: high for shipped decision/feedback architecture. - Caveats: no real LLM-agent run or measured repair effectiveness; after allowing localhost sockets for HTTP unit tests, 127 first-party tests passed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct agent-owned or constructed interference-damping function between independent Scout S1 units is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous tool execution can affect files/processes, but all calls originate from one model turn and are not independently deciding S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the single model chooses the multi-tool call set; executor threads only run and reassemble the calls. - Decision owner: same Agent model loop and deterministic thread-pool/registry dispatch. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paralleltools, ThreadPoolExecutor, ordered result IDs, synchronous bus event delivery, /fork session view copying. - Closure path: tool results return to the same S1; no independent-S1 conflict signal → dedicated coordination action → distinct S1 behavior change is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrent tools and forked conversation storage are not concurrently operating independent agents; result ordering is implementation consistency rather than S2 interference control. - Evidence: scout/agent.py; scout/builtin/session.py; tests/testagent.py. - Basis: explicit plus full small-source negative review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users could add independently operated agents with custom plugins; their unseen behavior is outside this first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no metasystem-level live resource/commitment/prioritization control over a portfolio of S1 units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: turn limits, provider retries and plugin failures are one-session technical constraints rather than organizational current-management conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user starts/resumes sessions and selects provider/tool settings; no whole-current management decision actor. - Decision owner: one operating agent and operator CLI, without separate S3 responsibility. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: max turns, config layering, REPL session commands, statusline, token usage and event log. - Closure path: session/tool feedback drives the same S1; no all-units current view → management priority/resource intervention → returned operation change circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model changing tasks and operator configuration are local operations, not substantive S3 current-control ownership, even under a parent-mode test. - Evidence: scout/host.py; scout/cli.py; scout/builtin/statusline.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repo-local Topo human approval of a plan artifact is not an operator&#39;s whole-current portfolio management.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent checking of management or operational claims through complementary reality access and authorized corrective return is packaged. - Disturbance / variety regulated: test/tool errors, event history and human artifact comments can challenge local outputs but do not constitute independent organizational audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate auditor with mandate and corrective management return. - Decision owner: same operational model consumes tool errors; in checkout mode, a human can comment on an HTML plan using Topo. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event bus, persistent history, developer offline tests and Topo topowrite/topoopen/topofeedback review channel. - Closure path: tool results or human Topo notes reenter the existing task conversation; no independent claim-vs-reality audit → responsible S3 decision owner → oversight correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party Topo human feedback is genuinely separate from model output, but reviews a plan artifact, not independently assesses management claims against operational reality; development tests and logs are ordinary evidence. - Evidence: .agents/plugins/topo.py; scout/bus.py; scout/builtin/session.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review, including project-local review mode. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an externally designed independent auditor using plugin hooks could differ; none is shipped as Scout&#39;s own S3 circuit.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future-environment sensing and selected adaptation of the agent&#39;s enduring capabilities is closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new files, provider choices and project skills are present-context inputs; long-term external changes are not modeled and acted upon through a prospective capability loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator installs/edits skills/plugins or changes config; one agent chooses present tool calls. - Decision owner: external author/installer for durable capabilities and the current S1 for task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .agents/ skill discovery, plugin loading, config layering, persisted history, /fork, Topo plan artifacts. - Closure path: external instructions/plugins load at boot and may influence the present model; no observed future distinction → candidate capability changes → selected implementation → validated later return is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, provider switching and user-authored modules expand action possibilities without a first-party prospective adaptation decision owner. - Evidence: scout/builtin/skills.py; scout/host.py; scout/builtin/session.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a model could be asked to write a plugin as a coding task, but task output alone does not make a standing S4 adaptation cycle.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate purpose/identity-policy governance circuit is implemented in first-party Scout. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator-provided project instructions and tool/provider configuration shape allowed tasks but not ultimate organizational dilemmas. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user starts and directs coding work; no issue-to-policy authority-and-return channel. - Decision owner: external user/maintainer for repository purpose, plugin installation and optional model credentials. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md prompt injection, model/provider configuration, user-installed plugins, REPL commands and runtime error handling. - Closure path: settings and prompts affect S1 actions; no legitimate identity-policy question → authority deliberation → binding governance decision → operational return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: system prompt and installed plugin commands are externally chosen task constraints, not S5 policy adjudication; the absence of permission prompts does not establish a policy owner either. - Evidence: scout/builtin/prompt.py; scout/host.py; scout/cli.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: real maintainer/user governance exists outside the declared agent runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/91bf76de3b2c253aea4705b85414c44044d608c5/assessments/sc/scout.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 91bf76de3b2c253aea4705b85414c44044d608c5\">91bf76d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T20:47:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/moloo4ni/few\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Few</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/moloo4ni/few/tree/c7dd1b9f6eeec1c4af7e3a0ee7c80132cf2b06be\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c7dd1b9f6eeec1c4af7e3a0ee7c80132cf2b06be\">c7dd1b9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: The native model-directed coding loop edits, inspects and tests the current user project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Code/test failures, changing user requirements, unfamiliar files and tool result variability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose read/write/edit/shell tools, inspect their returns and decide repair/finish. - Decision owner: A single configured LLM acting inside Few&#39;s first-party Rust Agent, with authority bounded by runtime permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Four native tools, asynchronous model streaming, task history, mode prompts, permission engine, automatic verify feedback. - Closure path: User request → agent provider reply with native tool calls → Few handlers operate on project files/shell → tool result appended to model conversation → next model action; post-edit verify failure also returns evidence to that same operating loop. - Boundary reachability: src/main.rs/src/app.rs construct the packaged executable agent whose Agent::run in src/agent/mod.rs invokes native tool dispatch in src/agent/exec.rs; no third-party coding agent loop is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses contingent coding actions. Human approvals and Rust permission checks enforce rather than choose the detailed operational response. - Evidence: src/agent/mod.rs; src/agent/exec.rs; src/tools.rs; src/main.rs; src/app.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Model is a configured external inference provider; live task success rates are not established by structural source evidence..\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct coordination relation among at least two code-producing S1 units exists at the distributed standard Few coding-session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The standard run has one decision-making coder. Sequential read/write/edit/shell calls do not create a second autonomous operational work cell whose behavior could oscillate or collide with a peer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No inter-S1 collision decision exists. The user&#39;s sequential command stream and deterministic tool policy are within single S1 execution. - Decision owner: Absent in the selected single-agent packaged coding runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One native tool dispatcher, permission gates, single conversation and user-input queues. - Closure path: Tools and turn results return to one agent; this is S1 local feedback, not inter-S1 stabilization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tasks, tools, queued user messages and modes are not distinct independently acting S1 units. - Evidence: src/agent/mod.rs; src/agent/exec.rs; src/tools.rs; src/app.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Separate unrelated Few processes could edit the same workspace, but first-party coordination across those processes is not demonstrated or credited..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No metasystem controller that observes several active S1 commitments and makes substantive inside-and-now resource/priority decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Budget steps, verify failures, session interruptions and approvals affect the current single coding task, not the current performance/priority balance of a multi-S1 organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runtime applies preset step ceilings and repeated-failure stop rules; user grants tool rights and may stop execution. - Decision owner: Deterministic runtime / human task operator; no distinct first-party agent that owns S3 judgement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskOutcome, RetryTracker, RunCtx::steplimitreached, TUI progress/status and saved sessions. - Closure path: Ceiling stops the task or verify failure returns to the same coder; no independent whole-current regulatory decision flows back to multiple S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A single agent&#39;s retry limit or refusal is local quality/safety enforcement rather than central current-system supervision. - Evidence: src/agent/mod.rs; src/agent/verify.rs; src/app.rs; src/session.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A wider human engineering team can supervise Few, but that external parent is not implemented as a first-party S3 mode..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Automatic tests provide task-local checking and repair prompts but no complementary independently owned audit organization of operating S1. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coder could leave incorrect edits; independent tests offer evidence of failure but the same model receives them as ordinary task feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The runtime chooses a configured or autodetected shell command, checks exit status, and sends failure text to the original coding model; it does not give a separate agent/auditor a material independent judgment or audit discretion. - Decision owner: Deterministic resolveverify, ordinary permission-gated shell test and same S1 coding actor; no independent S3 audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Auto cargo test, go test, npm test, pytest resolution; error signature and bounded repeated-failure tracker. - Closure path: Changed files → finishtextturn invokes ordinary shell verification → when it fails, [few verify] failure is appended to the same model&#39;s conversation → same S1 fixes or stops after threshold. This is honest local S1 feedback but lacks complementary independent audit ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Verification is not automatically S3: only the same agent repairs and the deterministic check has no independent audit mandate/organizational autonomy. - Evidence: src/agent/mod.rs; src/agent/verify.rs; src/agent/exec.rs; src/perms.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: If a user&#39;s project supplies strong tests, those can be useful external evaluation evidence but do not make Few itself own an independent complementary audit actor..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party forward-looking environment study and strategic renewal authority beyond local memory, context compaction and task-directed edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future tooling, market, capability or environment changes would require prospective sensing, alternatives and deliberate organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Memory files and context summaries preserve previously seen facts; the user owns project goals/model selection. - Decision owner: No prospective S4 decision owner in standard shipped runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Markdown global/project memory, context compaction triggered by token usage, session restore, configuration and plan mode. - Closure path: Notes and summaries may inform later turns, but do not generate/evaluate future environmental alternatives then enact an organizational capability change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Storing context and preserving sessions is not by itself future intelligence/renewal. - Evidence: src/memory.rs; src/agent/compact.rs; src/session.rs; src/config.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: The actual software repository may evolve under human maintainers; its development organization is outside the installed coding-agent recursion..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No highest-level mission/identity-purpose decision and policy ratification closure for an autonomous coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool safety, path trust and auto-approve modes affect current action authority rather than the organization&#39;s constitutive policy/identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user chooses mode and scope and grants/denies individual tool rights; deterministic policy engine enforces. - Decision owner: Human owner of local preferences, not a first-party S5 governance actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mode::Plan|Build|Auto, capability matrix, sensitive-path deny list, durable user grants. - Closure path: Human approval → action allowed/denied, or mode changes tool posture; no identity/purpose issue is deliberated and returned as a binding policy decision for the whole system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Capability enforcement or plan mode does not establish legitimate ultimate organizational authority. - Evidence: src/perms.rs; src/config.rs; src/app.rs; src/sysprompt.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The maintainers&#39; software licensing and governance are separate external systems, not shipped end-user coding policy..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3ed045cca7223ac4dd6bc55aa5455fb752811b2e/assessments/few.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3ed045cca7223ac4dd6bc55aa5455fb752811b2e\">3ed045c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T16:13:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/clebert/drinky\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Drinky</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/clebert/drinky/tree/868f50c2604a5619238c6235cd63820d9c724c38\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 868f50c2604a5619238c6235cd63820d9c724c38\">868f50c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository coding actions with open-ended model decisions revised from current tool observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source structure, ambiguous implementation steps, tool errors, changing operator intent, provider failures and tool-result variance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select which source operations/commands to attempt next and whether to revise them from returned results. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Drinky Agent instance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: seven tool implementations, provider-neutral transport, output/time bounds, tool-call completion receipts, retry/steering and compiled context instructions. - Closure path: user objective + tool specifications → model selects actions → read/search/edit/write/bash execution → recorded tool results → subsequent model call or stop. - Boundary reachability: the Agent loop and bundled tool registry are reachable from the shipped single Zig CLI/TUI executable and optional paired chat interface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without a model, static Zig tool functions cannot choose arbitrary coding actions or adapt to tool evidence; the model owns those next-action decisions. - Evidence: lib/ai/Agent.zig, tool/root.zig, src/App.zig. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the selected provider can fail or limit reachability; source inspection establishes executable path, not effectiveness with a live LLM.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no independent S1-to-S1 interference attenuation with corrective coordination feedback is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent read/search tool scheduling and ordered write barriers occur inside one model actor, not across autonomous operational units. - Distinct S1 units: only one first-party model-backed operating Agent; read-only tool threads do not own separate coding agendas. - Inter-S1 disturbance: none among a population of separately acting shipped S1 units at this boundary. - Attenuating coordination relation: fixed batching/barriers prevent intra-agent tool races but are not an inter-S1 conflict regulator. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: tool results return to the same parent model; no S2-specific conflict finding and altered relations among distinct S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate S2 decision found. - Decision owner: no S2 owner; the single Agent and static executor own task decisions and scheduling respectively. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-onlycallsmax, mutating tool barriers, bounded tool rounds and result-slot accounting. - Closure path: tool actions → serialized/concurrent dispatch → same-Agent result feedback exists, but multiple S1 conflict → S2 regulation → changed distinct-unit behavior is absent. - Boundary reachability: the batching code is shipped; a multi-agent coordination mode is not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dynamic choice of task actions does not turn deterministic per-batch tool scheduling into organizational S2. - Evidence: Agent.zig, tool/root.zig, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external users could run several separate Drinky processes, but the installed product does not wire their independent work into one S2 control loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current allocation/commitment management across independently accountable S1 operating units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current turn failure/cancellation, provider quota and the attached remote UI do not constitute a multiactor operating portfolio. - Whole-system current view: per-process transcript/status, account/model/usage and optional Herdr or Telegram mirror show one session only. - Current-control decision scope: operator may steer, cancel, resume/retry or switch models for this one coding session, without a separately tasked current-resource manager. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S3 current control right evidenced. - Decision owner: single S1 agent and human operator of a selected session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent receipt, account quota display, state file, paired bot and Herdr event reports. - Closure path: process state → user-visible feedback → task steering exists; current whole-organization bargaining over S1 commitments is not wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the remote controller owns network attachment and delivers user actions but does not allocate resources among independent operational units. - Evidence: remote/Controller.zig, src/State.zig, Agent.zig. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a human can manage several sessions externally; this assessment does not import an external human organization as an internal Drinky S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary audit judgment with sufficiently independent operational access and returned corrective decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding claims could be incorrect, but the product does not ship a separate reviewer or independent verification control path. - Claim being audited: whether model-proposed code edits and reported outcomes are correct. - Ordinary reporting path: the same Agent records tool stdout/stderr and its own model responses in one transcript. - Complementary access path: no shipped independent review actor or direct alternative evidence channel beyond ordinary read/search/bash observations and user-authored skills. - Independence boundary: the transcript, tool output and any self-analysis all belong to the working Agent; external human review is not a first-party audit mode. - Audit judgment / decisive feedback right: no separate independent risk or correctness verdict owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S3-specific judgment right evidenced. - Decision owner: main coding Agent for current actions; optional user review remains external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: transcript result slots, tool-error reports, source inspection tools and developer test scripts. - How findings enter subsequent control: tool results inform the model, but no separately produced independent audit finding is returned to current control. - Who acts on findings: the Agent reacts to ordinary tool feedback, subject to user steering. - Closure path: code action → its own tool observation → model continuation exists; separate audit → corrective return is absent. - Boundary reachability: no built-in reviewer/subagent workflow was found in the shipped seven-tool registry and single-agent loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can critique its own work if prompted, but it has no complementary independent audit mandate and access distinct from its normal task path. - Evidence: Agent.zig, tool/root.zig, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository scripts/test-audit.sh is developer test infrastructure, not the runtime&#39;s complementary organizational reviewer.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future-facing environmental intelligence loop evaluating and adopting new organizational capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider quotas, live catalog changes, project-specific model choice and prompt-history reuse concern current tool availability and user continuity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous prospective capability planning/adoption decision. - Decision owner: operator chooses provider/model and edits configuration/skills, while providers supply catalog metadata. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider model list refresh, quota and cost state, prompt history, model selection and developer backlog. - Closure path: current service metadata → displayed choices/current model update exists; horizon scan → prospective capability option → adoption/change in organizational control does not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: refreshing remote provider metadata and remembering chosen models do not give a first-party adaptation actor decision ownership. - Evidence: src/State.zig, lib/ai/Catalog.zig, BACKLOG.md. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: developers may later implement roadmap features; frozen planned work is not current product S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installed ultimate organizational identity/purpose decision and authoritative return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local tool risks and workspace trust are not referred into a Drinky constitutional policy forum. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity-level policy controversy adjudication evidenced. - Decision owner: external user/configuration/developers choose instructions and limits; no first-party S5 authority is installed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: small system prompt, user AGENTS.md/skills, configured bash time limits and request bounds, external container advice. - Closure path: prompt/configuration → Agent action constraints is present; ultimate identity dispute → legitimate policy decision → authoritative adoption feedback is absent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the README explicitly states there is no runtime permission gate/sandbox; even an added one would not alone establish ultimate identity/policy governance. - Evidence: README.md, systemprompt.zig, Config.zig. - Basis: explicit + structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: legitimate human decisions over external project rules exist, but no in-boundary whole-organization ultimate-policy closure was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2c5551331344622f554decf14951e1d559285afe/assessments/dr/drinky.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2c5551331344622f554decf14951e1d559285afe\">2c55513 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T17:43:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/startupmini/minicode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Minicode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/startupmini/minicode/tree/aa76dfbea2c3d262b7ff4951e532277135793487\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision aa76dfbea2c3d262b7ff4951e532277135793487\">aa76dfb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform software-engineering work in the selected workspace through a model-owned sequence of inspection, editing, execution and evidence-driven revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, repository structure, source-code defects, tool/shell outcomes, provider failures, context pressure, permission restrictions and test feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next permitted coding/tool action from current task context and returned observations, revise the approach after errors/results and decide when to produce a terminal response. - Decision owner: the model-backed Minicode coding actor running through the vendored MiniCore loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party tool registry, permission handler, sandbox policy, bounded executor, provider recovery, context compaction, sessions/checkpoints, todo/memory surfaces and optional verification. - Closure path: user objective + current session/workspace evidence → model selects tool/action → Minicode permission/executor path runs it → tool result is committed to the turn context → next model step observes it → revised action or terminal answer. - Boundary reachability: this is the ordinary Minicode interactive/headless execution path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves deterministic transport, policy and execution machinery, but removes the open-ended choice of coding actions and revisions. - Evidence: pinned vendored loop.ts; src/app/session.ts; src/tools/index.ts; README. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference is external infrastructure; the credited organizational discretion is the first-party model/tool loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no same-recursion inter-S1 coordination loop meeting the Profile threshold is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: delegated children can run concurrently, and concurrent mutating work in one parent workspace could in principle interfere; however the production delegation path does not expose a concrete first-party relation that detects/negotiates/attenuates such collisions and feeds the result into subsequent peer behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pool limits simultaneous delegated children; child scopes restrict tools/permissions; child journals isolate evidence; parent receives child summaries/events. - Closure path: absent at S2 level. The pool supplies a capacity limit, not disturbance-specific coordination among operational children. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing to delegate and returning child summaries is task decomposition. Children do not negotiate shared-workspace conflict, reserve edit regions or receive a coordination decision that changes their later behavior. - Evidence: src/tools/task.ts; src/agents/pool.ts; cli/index.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: independent child sessions are real S1-like operational actors, but multiplicity alone is not S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control owner is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current session steps, child concurrency, budgets, permissions, todo state and background processes are bounded/observable, but no actor has a whole-current operational portfolio plus discretionary authority over shared commitments/resources on behalf of a multi-S1 whole. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: max steps/timeouts, sub-agent pool limit, rate limiting, permissions, todo state, journals, session state and cancellation. - Closure path: no whole-system current view → resource/priority/commitment intervention → changed multi-S1 operation loop was reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent agent&#39;s choice to invoke a child and consume its result remains decomposition. Static/bounded runtime gates enforce constraints rather than choose whole-system current policy. - Evidence: vendored kernel session/loop; src/tools/task.ts; src/agents/pool.ts; cli/setup.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this negative mapping does not deny that Minicode exposes useful supervisory telemetry or limits; it means those controls do not establish the Profile&#39;s distinct whole-system S3 function at the reviewed recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: faulty code or incorrect completion claims can be caught by opt-in verification, tests, LSP diagnostics and other checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate independent audit judgment owner is established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: --verify, baseline verification, deterministic verification commands, completion-evidence reconciliation, test/LSP output and self-heal cycles. - Closure path: verification evidence does return into further coding, but failed verification is fed back to the same Minicode coding session for self-heal. This is routine operational QA rather than a complementary independent audit channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no separate reviewer/model/evaluator with materially different access to operational reality and its own audit judgment. - Evidence: cli/setup.ts runPromptWithVerify / runPromptWithVerifyInner. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic test evidence can be strong operational verification, but independence is the missing organizational property; the same coding session owns the corrective judgment after failures.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective adaptation loop that changes future Minicode capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current-task web/MCP information, memory retrieval, verified-run snippets and provider/model changes can influence later work, but none of these surfaces establishes the required environmental sensing → adaptation-option development → returned capability-change loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web tools, MCP, memory RAG, session persistence, verified-run snippet memory, model/provider selection and repo-map/context loading. - Closure path: no first-party prospective adaptation option is shown changing Minicode&#39;s reusable capability/configuration through an S4 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory and current-task research preserve/use evidence but do not themselves make future-oriented capability adaptations. - Evidence: README; src/policy/context.ts; memory/session surfaces; verify snippet behavior in cli/setup.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: durable memory and externally sourced task evidence may improve later answers, but no first-party mechanism on this frozen ref converts them into a prospective change of reusable Minicode capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the assessed harness boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, sandboxing and project context constrain coding behavior, but constraints are not by themselves S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party identity-level decision path with legitimate ultimate authority and returned governance was reconstructed. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt, permission modes, sandbox policy, trusted/local configuration, operator approval and project context files. - Closure path: ordinary approvals/configuration alter execution posture, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue is escalated through a qualifying first-party parent loop and returned to govern subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no agent or qualifying first-party parent owns an identity-level decision path here. buildSystemPrompt explicitly marks MEMORY, repo map, skills, steering and agent files as untrusted data that “never override the instructions above”; AGENTS/CLAUDE/steering therefore do not act as authoritative parent constitutions. - Evidence: src/policy/context.ts; src/policy/permission.ts; src/app/session.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human approval remains meaningful execution authority, but the inspected approvals are action-level decisions rather than an identity/ultimate-policy loop with a returned governance decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fbddb014408c105900f624a62057902915a99ec1/assessments/minicode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fbddb014408c105900f624a62057902915a99ec1\">fbddb01 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T21:55:00Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zayokami/labunbun-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LaBunbun Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zayokami/labunbun-code/tree/1470556287af8325a2c7971f7858f42c0d8241a2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1470556287af8325a2c7971f7858f42c0d8241a2\">1470556 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a user coding objective into repository/process changes or a task result through model-selected tool actions with returned environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation alternatives, file/shell/tool results, model uncertainty, provider/context failures, permission outcomes and evolving task evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next task-specific tool call or substantive answer and revise later choices from returned tool/environment evidence. - Decision owner: the configured model actor running inside the first-party parent or nested AgentSession. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSession; first-party coding tools and pipeline; session store; compaction/recovery; permission engine; task list; skills/MCP; nested Task tool; background shell controls; provider adapters. - Closure path: current prompt/history/environment evidence → model emits tool call or answer → first-party runtime permits/executes the action → tool/environment result is appended to the session → same model actor observes the result and chooses another action or final output. - Boundary reachability: the documented labunbun interactive and labunbun -p headless entrypoints instantiate the shipped AgentSession directly. Task subagents instantiate the same first-party operational loop through the standard Task tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining tools, permission rules, task storage, concurrency and session machinery removes the substantive task-specific judgment of what action to take next; those mechanisms support or constrain the decision rather than replacing it. - Evidence: packages/agent/src/session.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/main.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/headless.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference and external services remain dependencies; the positive finding is for first-party closure of their task-specific decisions through the repository/tool loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific inter-S1 disturbance-attenuation loop is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete interaction-generated conflict, oscillation or coupled disturbance among sibling operational S1s is detected and attenuated by a dedicated first-party relation. - Distinct S1 units: Task calls can instantiate distinct nested AgentSession operational units with separate contexts and tool loops. - Inter-S1 disturbance: not established as a concrete product-observed relation. Sibling subagents can share a workspace/resource envelope, but the frozen runtime does not identify a specific sibling collision/contention condition as the input to a coordination decision. - Attenuating coordination relation: not established. The generic tool semaphore/batching cap limits simultaneous concurrency-safe calls; it does not resolve semantic/file/commit conflicts or otherwise coordinate one sibling S1 in response to another sibling&#39;s state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: not established for an inter-S1 disturbance. Task results return to the parent after completion, while semaphore admission merely delays generic safe calls until capacity is available. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is not. The inspected positive-looking surfaces are delegation, batching and generic execution-capacity control, so they are not promoted to S2 without a concrete disturbance-specific attenuation/feedback witness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task subagents, partitionToolCalls, per-turn Semaphore, task-list dependencies/status and parent collection of final subagent reports. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model can choose to delegate and consume results as part of its own S1 task pursuit; no separate S2 function is shown regulating interaction among sibling S1s. - Evidence: packages/coding-agent/src/subagents.ts; packages/agent/src/concurrency.ts; packages/tools/src/tasks.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream composition could impose coordination among several LaBunbun sessions, but generic fan-out or a concurrency cap is not credited without the Methodology 0.3.6 disturbance-and-feedback witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control loop is established above the current coding task/session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-list progress, subagent completion, background-process status, permissions and session/context state are maintained locally, but not through a distinct higher-recursion current-control authority over a population of autonomous operational commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct whole-system owner with authority to reprioritize, reallocate or intervene across current S1 commitments is established. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parent AgentSession, task list, nested Task results, background shell manager, event stream, session persistence/resume, plan mode and concurrency controls. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Whole-system current view: the task list exposes the decomposition/status of one coding objective and the parent eventually receives final Task reports; no boundary-reachable surface maintains a live whole-population view of all autonomous subagents plus commitments/resources for a separate control decision. - Current-control decision scope: the operating model may choose its own next task/action/delegation as part of S1. Task status bookkeeping, generic concurrency admission and a human plan-approval gate do not establish a separate S3 resource/priority/commitment authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same model pursuing the user task can read/update the task list and decide to spawn a subagent, but that is decomposition inside its operating objective rather than a distinct metasystem S3 function. - Evidence: packages/tools/src/tasks.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/subagents.ts; packages/tools/src/background.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/plan-mode.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the parent is an orchestrating S1 and can make delegation choices; orchestration naming or task-board visibility alone is not sufficient for S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit loop with sufficiently independent access and returned corrective closure is established in the standard product runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: configurable hooks, code-review skills, permission checks and plan approval can inspect or constrain actions, but no first-party complementary auditor independently observes actual S1 operation and returns a qualifying audit finding into subsequent organizational control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PreToolUse/PostToolUse/Stop and other command hooks, permissions, plan approval, project trust, code-review skills and normal tool/test output. - Closure path: not applicable; no standard path closes ordinary operational evidence → complementary independent access → audit finding → returned corrective organizational decision. - Claim being audited: not established as a distinct operational claim under an independent complementary channel. - Ordinary reporting path: assistant/tool results, task/session events, subagent final reports, background logs and persisted session data. - Complementary access path: configurable command hooks are the strongest candidate. PreToolUse can block before an action, but that is an inline configured guard; PostToolUse is explicitly advisory after execution and receives tool name/input rather than an independent result/evidence channel. Review skills run through the ordinary model/tool composition rather than a distinct audit boundary. - Independence boundary: external hook commands can be separate processes, but their content/criteria are user-configured and the standard distribution does not establish a complementary observer with independent evidence access and a required return path over actual operations. - Who acts on findings: a PreToolUse hook may cause the ordinary tool call to be blocked; advisory post-use failures are reported. No qualifying independent audit finding is shown returning to a separate current-control owner for corrective action. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hooks and review prompts may provide downstream checks when configured, but the product supplies an extension point rather than owning a complete S3 function. - Evidence: packages/coding-agent/src/hooks.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/headless.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/plan-mode.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a particular user can configure an external verifier hook, but user-authored downstream composition is not credited as first-party S3 ownership without the required standard complementary closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material autonomous prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: memory/session state, context pressure, provider failures and imported/user-authored configuration are handled, but not through an actor that detects an external future-relevant change, generates an organizational adaptation and persists it back as a new current capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory/rules loading, session persistence/resume, context compaction, skills/agent definitions, model fallback, provider catalog probing, migration/import tooling and settings. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - External distinction: provider/tool/repository conditions can be observed during ordinary work, but no S4-specific environmental sensing surface classifies a future-relevant organizational change. - Future distinction: not established beyond ordinary task planning or provider-recovery handling. - Adaptation option: operator-authored settings/skills/agents/rules and imported configurations can change capability, but the frozen product does not autonomously generate and select such a durable option from external evidence. - Path back to current capability: memory/settings/definitions are loaded on later runs when humans or external tooling write them; no first-party autonomous S4 loop is shown persisting its own selected adaptation and then operating under it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operating model may reason about future steps inside one coding task, while durable skills/rules/settings remain externally authored or imported. Context compaction and model fallback preserve service, not organizational adaptation. - Evidence: packages/coding-agent/src/memory.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/subagents.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: persistence and extensibility are substantial, but Methodology 0.3.6 requires the future-facing adaptation decision and return-to-capability closure, not merely a place where a human can store instructions.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity or ultimate-policy authority loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission rules, project-definition trust, plan approval and configured system/memory instructions constrain what the coding agent may do, but ultimate policy remains authored or approved outside the autonomous runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an autonomous identity/ultimate-policy owner. - Decision owner: user/operator and externally supplied configuration for the strongest candidate policy/identity decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes/rules, user approval dialogs, plan mode, project-definition trust ledger, settings, system prompts, memory/rules, skills and agent definitions. - Closure path: not applicable for an autonomous S5 finding; configured/human policy is loaded and enforced by code rather than reconsidered by a runtime ultimate-policy actor. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether repository-supplied definitions are trusted, which tools/actions are allowed and whether an implementation plan may leave read-only mode are policy questions, but their decisive authority is explicitly external. - Ultimate authority: the user/operator and layered configuration/rule sources. The permission engine deterministically enforces those decisions; it does not decide the organization&#39;s own ultimate identity or policy premise. - Return-to-operation path: user approvals or configured rules directly change what ordinary operation may do (for example, plan approval restores the prior permission mode), but this is an external governance gate rather than autonomous S5 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the user/configured rule sources leaves the enforcement mechanism without the ultimate policy choices. The model cannot autonomously approve repository definitions, rewrite deny rules or self-authorize leaving plan mode under the standard path. - Evidence: packages/agent/src/permissions.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/plan-mode.ts; packages/coding-agent/src/project-trust.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong fail-closed permission/trust mechanics improve safety but enforcement of externally authored policy is not itself S5 decision ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c61af124518d10afba059851784a929742f255c/assessments/labunbun-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c61af124518d10afba059851784a929742f255c\">3c61af1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T02:36:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chumingjun/dsh-harness-one\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow One</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chumingjun/dsh-harness-one/tree/08aef04761e8c6586efaa4077b49d349d4f0f920\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 08aef04761e8c6586efaa4077b49d349d4f0f920\">08aef04 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: External-facing, model-guided execution of agent nodes as units of work inside an operator-authored/assistant-authored DAG workflow; produce per-node deliverables and downstream task feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Varying user/trigger input, missing or inconsistent upstream outputs, unexpected tool/HTTP/Feishu contents, model decisions during a node, node crashes and timeout, unsupported dsh runtime availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured LLM determines agent-node reasoning, tool selection and tool arguments through repeated execution feedback. An installed dsh headless runtime can execute a prompted node; a first-party built-in OpenAI/Anthropic-compatible LLM tool loop, or node-level Plan→Execute→Summarize path, runs when selected or as fallback. Each node task can react to incoming upstream outputs. - Decision owner: Configured LLM/dsh headless agent under a graph node identity; the workflow author defines node prompt, model/channel/tool subset and graph dependencies, and deterministic Workflow One runner admits/distributes work. The model owns the next local task action in supported live model mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orchestrator agent-node dispatch and output extraction, dsh runtime detection/spawn/fallback, node-level routed provider LLM tool calls, first-party tool executor/workspace and skills, plan-mode optional model plan and execution, optional plugin-mode graph execution. - Closure path: User/cron/webhook/chat triggered graph → ready agent node with explicit upstream result and task prompt → LLM-guided tool call / dsh headless task → tool or shell result observed by runtime model → delivered text/files, structured results and node state → downstream nodes receive success/output; error/retry/cancel follows configured graph runtime. - Boundary reachability: Shipped server files initialize Orchestrator with createLLM() and createToolExecutor; runAgentNode uses an optional reachable dsh executable or executes llm.chat(...) against selected real configured provider and tools. Source-backed plugin engine independently accepts agent NodeKind execution callbacks and packages persistent run result fields. Model path is separate from MockLLM fallback. No external dsh internal autonomy is assumed without first-party invocation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model agent choices while keeping DAG and timeout rules leaves a static task runner and MockLLM deterministic demonstration, not a model-guided response to unseen data. This proves a live-capable S1 feedback right within bounded agent nodes, not autonomous ownership of the entire enterprise. - Evidence: server/orchestrator.js; server/agent-runtime.js; server/llm.js; server/tools.js; server/plan-mode.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/engine.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/index.js; server/workspace.js. - Basis: Pin-specific first-party production entry wiring and provider adapters, not a measured real model deployment run. - Confidence: High for agent-node task autonomy; no paid external LLM/GPU workload executed. - Caveats: Multiple agents are role-specific transient graph nodes of one predesigned business workflow. External host dsh headless internals are not reassessed. A separate MockLLM demonstrates wiring but is not an autonomous source.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced S2 function balancing interactions among separately viable S1 business units: DAG fan-out/dataflow, concurrent node readiness and result routing are orchestration of finite task parts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two graph nodes can execute in parallel, require prior outputs, face failed predecessors or compete for execution slots; output formats or variable names may conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runner deterministically decides ready nodes from dependency counters, concurrency slots, graph branch conditions, on-error downstream skips and shared-output templates; AI canvas assistant may edit one graph when specifically commanded. No independently endowed reciprocal interdepartmental conflict damping or negotiated coordination decision is shown. - Decision owner: Graph owner chooses edges, tools and conditions; JS DAG engine/runner enforces them. No independent S2 decision agent governing distinct, viable, continuing business S1 units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Multi-run execution map, pump readiness, dependency counters, graph lint, template-variable scoping and subworkflow nodes, concurrency/timeout, node notification. - Closure path: A node finishes/fails and engine routes predefined output/success/failure to downstream nodes; this is finite workflow dataflow and scheduler feedback rather than an inter-S1 coordination regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent node != continuing viable departmental operation. The existence of many role-labeled models or parallel node execution does not alone demonstrate independent enduring operation, mutual interference assessment, or a distinct coordinating feedback mechanism mediating S1-to-S1 tensions. - Evidence: server/orchestrator.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/engine.js; server/template.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/subworkflow.js; README.en.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting both legacy server and distributed dsh plugin-mode scheduler, direct-upstream template admission and shared graph controls. - Confidence: High at public packaged graph-worker boundary. - Caveats: One could deploy independent business departments behind adapter nodes and construct organizational S2 above them; neither deployment nor returned coordination mandate is established by this repository.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No agent-owned manager of several separately viable S1 units&#39; current organizational resource/priority/commitment portfolio, despite strong deterministic DAG-wide run control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Many node tasks contend for concurrency, queued nodes fail/timeout, interrupted graphs may need resume, operators need visibility and cancel/retry. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Orchestrator/plugin engine grants predefined DAG scheduling, concurrency caps, abort, timeout, resume/recover, status tracking, and failure propagation. These are programmed runtime execution policies; AI canvas editing can alter a particular graph in response to the current user&#39;s request. Neither is evidenced as an autonomous/parent-governed standing S3 manager with independent discretion and returned current multi-department decisions. - Decision owner: Host workflow author/operator and deterministic JS execution engine; model tool session may patch graph as an agent executing a bounded user request, not act as a separately delegated organization-wide S3 current controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run/node statuses, scheduling slots, GraphOps lint, branch expression and output contract validation, restart/resume, SSE events, manual retry/cancel, cron triggers and per-node tool budgets. - Closure path: Configured current graph → node scheduling/state observations → programmed next-node firing/retry/cancel based on user-specified policy. AI edited graph commits remain user-requested workflow changes; no collective viable-S1 departmental condition → autonomous whole-current decision/parent mandate → accountability/resource allocation returned to distinct operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A complete DAG current-state view is instrumentation, not by itself a discretionary inside-and-now governance owner. The current run is a finite task graph, not a set of independently viable departments. Editing a graph on a chat instruction does not confer enduring management powers, and human toolbar approvals do not make S3=P without a function-specific parent decision/return contract. - Evidence: dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/engine.js; server/orchestrator.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/assistant.js; server/server.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/index.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/schedule.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/output-contract.js. - Basis: Bounded negative after evaluating plausible whole-run scheduler, monitoring controls and bounded AI graph authoring as S3 candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Model may construct a capable future S3 system using graph primitives, but this assessment does not promote generic constructor affordances into an already established S3=C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of the actuality of management-wide operating conditions challenging a distinct S3 decision owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Runs can fail silently, upstream outputs can be incorrect, model-generated actions might omit expected files, interrupted state may differ from operator perception. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runtime emits SSE node-status/result/trace and retains execution metadata; graph lint and typed output contract can flag malformed nodes/outputs, notification plugin relays statuses. None credibly exercises an independent audit mandate over current managerial claims using a distinct evidence route with correction returned to S3. - Decision owner: Deterministic graph validator, execution engine, operator UI/history and notifications; no designated independently authorized S3 decision or S3 who receives an audit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Graph lint, run history, node execution trace, artifacts, schema validator, failure and resume states, notifications and user viewing. - Closure path: Output/task events → storage/SSE UI/history for current task inspection and optional manual retry. No management claim → independent direct S1 field-verification → finding → S3 organizational corrective decision returned to S1s. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Instrumented run observability is real but not independent operational reality auditing. Typed-result checking is local validation. No separate external access/auditor authority challenges the whole-current management model. - Evidence: dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/engine.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/output-contract.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/notifications.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/assistant.js; server/orchestrator.js; server/server.js. - Basis: Bounded absence after checking observability, retry, notifications, validation and independent evidence/source of current organizational truth. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Operators can audit node histories themselves, but the code does not establish a first-party independent S3 function or a closed parent manager response.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No continuous first-party outside-and-then intelligence that forms future strategic options for a continuing viable operating system and returns accepted adaptation into ongoing capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User needs new business workflows, external events can trigger runs, model/provider failures and changing inputs require task/path planning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A chat-connected agent can propose and patch a canvas graph, and an optional Plan→Execute→Summarize node chooses steps within a present task. Cron/webhook/Feishu events trigger configured workflows, while graph templates make new workflow designs easy. No shipped autonomous future environment/research horizon, strategic option adjudication or independent S4 decision+return loop was found. - Decision owner: Current user/agent session chooses graph modifications for its explicit present request; graph maintainer/operator owns lasting workflow deployment. Fixed triggers/cron re-run prior graph. No distinct continuing foresight/adaptation owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AI canvas assistant canvas patch tools with lint/undo, current-task plan prompt, workflow generation/edit UI and templates, cron scheduling/misfire, provider fallbacks. - Closure path: User request → bounded AI patch proposal/tool/graph validation → UI canvas update/undo; configured future trigger → same workflow run. No environmental intelligence outside current case → future system-adaptation option → accepted decision → new current capability/whole-operational remit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Graph creation is useful first-party agentic design of current requested workflows, not independent management of a longer-term strategic portfolio. The runtime does not consume retrospective workflow outcomes to propose and accept new future strategy; static calendars do not supply external foresight. - Evidence: dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/assistant.js; server/plan-mode.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/schedule.js; README.en.md; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/index.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/engine.js. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting AI canvas graph patching, execution history and automatic future scheduling as strongest candidate S4 paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external user may request a forward-looking DAG or manually adapt future capabilities; that could be a separate supported use case in an organization, but the published first-party loop itself is request-driven, not an independent organizational S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No recognized first-party ultimate identity, mission or constitutional-policy authority at an organizational boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operators must control allowed tools, credentials, private file paths, app account/Feishu integration and which user actions may create/modify/run workflows. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Graph operators set node permissions and endpoint configuration; credentials are stored in plugin integration; external humans choose workflows, cancel/resume, edit canvas; security/path and lint policies block malformed graph operations. No legitimate top-level identity/purpose decision owner is named with a returned constitutional mandate. - Decision owner: Outside human project/workflow owner and host app configuration; first-party tools enforce per-request capability or safety rules, not organizational S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool subsets, credential handling, graph validation, workspace path limits, event notification and approval/undo in UI, installed dsh profile, human workflow configuration. - Closure path: Developer/operator-configured rules → deterministic accepted/rejected graph/node operations or user-directed patch → further workflow tasks, rather than system identity/policy agenda → legitimate S5 decision → binding returned charter and operating governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An AI can edit canvas elements or plan task steps but cannot autonomously redefine legitimate purpose/identity of the deployment. A human UI and GitHub MIT license establish neither S5=P organizational governance nor an agent-owned S5=A. - Evidence: dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/assistant.js; server/tools.js; server/workspace.js; dsh-plugins/dsh-ccpg-orchestrator/lib/engine.js; server/server.js; README.en.md. - Basis: Bounded negative at shipped application/operator boundary, including normal human selection and configuration paths. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external human institution may have S5, but its constitutional decision/return is beyond this first-party graph product and no parent-composite grade is inferred.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a737906face164a5aed6647d982fd2da115a9d2e/assessments/ds/dsh-harness-one.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a737906face164a5aed6647d982fd2da115a9d2e\">a737906 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T18:20:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TaewoooPark/Motifcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Motifcode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TaewoooPark/Motifcode/tree/9fb0d50acf577f60d87592c38b3ae2b57bd9c523\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9fb0d50acf577f60d87592c38b3ae2b57bd9c523\">9fb0d50 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform an open-ended coding task through model-chosen inspection/edit/shell actions and observations fed to subsequent model turns. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown repository layout, changing file contents, rejected tool payloads, program output, failed commands and incomplete code. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next coding/tool action, arguments, repair response, or task completion from newly observed results. - Decision owner: model-directed first-party Motifcode coding actor; the model endpoint supplies inference, while runLoop and ToolExecutor structure and execute decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: channel/protocol codecs, schema validator, BreakageBudget, policies, journal/checkpoint, permissions, local file/shell tools and transport retry. - Closure path: user task → runLoop → transport.complete → parsed/validated action → executor.run → serializeObservation into session → next model turn / confirmed finish. - Boundary reachability: normal packaged direct and interactive CLI code invokes runLoop; tool execution is first-party and does not rely on a third-party coding harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the evolving model-selected coding operations are not a predetermined deterministic workflow; guardrails limit but do not author the task plan. - Evidence: packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/cli/src/main.ts, packages/cli/src/executor.ts. - Basis: explicit source-level model/tool/observation loop. - Confidence: high for implemented path; live provider behavior not executed. - Caveats: actual model performance, endpoint access and operator grants are outside this source-only result.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct S2 relation attenuating evidenced interference or oscillation among separate operational S1 divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent children competing for model endpoint capacity, queued work, possible child-task scope interactions; no independently established inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: S2 would require judgment or first-party relation that changes competing S1 behaviors in response to a specific shared disturbance; not evidenced here. - Decision owner: AgentScheduler applies a generic concurrency cap and FIFO-like waiting; parent model selects tasks and consumes children. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scheduler queue, concurrencyFor local/remote endpoint limits, child scope journal, per-child read-only execution. - Closure path: child finishes and returns result; scheduler releases capacity, but no S2-specific conflict-resolution decision with corrective return to distinct S1 units is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: capacity serialization and generic delegation are not by themselves S2-specific feedback over an identified inter-S1 interference. - Evidence: packages/agents/src/index.ts, packages/cli/src/chat.ts, packages/cli/src/main.ts. - Basis: bounded negative, source-first. - Confidence: moderate, at one-session recursion. - Caveats: user-designed multi-agent workflows could create distinct S1 interference, but are not source-proven here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately owned whole-session current-regulation function over commitments, priorities, resource allocations or system-wide operational tradeoffs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool failures, parse errors, turn caps, transport errors, completion claims, approval decisions and nested job capacity are local execution disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: substantive current whole-system operational commitment/constraint choices beyond the action loop; not located. - Decision owner: parent coding model executes task-level planning and delegation; operator grants tool permissions; deterministic loop enforces budgets and retries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BreakageBudget, LoopGuard, AgentScheduler, permission policy, abort and channel downgrade. - Closure path: local guard rejects or re-prompts an action; no independent whole-current S3 decision/feedback to subsequent subordinate S1 operation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: global task counter and self-correcting parser are local S1 execution management, not organizational S3 governance. - Evidence: packages/core/src/loop.ts, packages/core/src/budget.ts, packages/agents/src/index.ts, packages/cli/src/policy.ts. - Basis: bounded negative, tested against current-control interpretation. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: the parent model owns task selection, not a source-identified distinct whole-system regulatory mandate.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: an optional separately iterating reviewer child can perform grounded correctness review, but the evidence does not conclusively establish a first-party complementary audit organizational path with independent claim access and accountable findings disposition at the selected default session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unverified or incorrect code change claims, missed edge cases and defects in the producer&#39;s edited files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer may inspect code under read-only tools and report concrete failing inputs; parent receives the child&#39;s summary, but an audit-specific obligatory/recognized subsequent governance or repair outcome is not demonstrated. - Decision owner: optional dedicated child model reviewer decides findings using a distinct system prompt/tool subset; parent main coding model chooses whether to invoke and how to act. It is a separate invocation, though neither automatic trigger nor independent organizational authority follows from the name. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in reviewer task definition, task dispatch, per-child runLoop and journal scope, read-only policy, result summary and failure handling. - Closure path: parent voluntarily requests reviewer task → reviewer operates code-inspection tools → child done summary → ToolExecutor.run returns a tool observation to parent. Actual independent audit findings reaching S3 or an accountable remedial decision are not established from this generic optional path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: optional reviewer owns analysis within its child task, but the shipped ordinary mode does not prove complementary audit-function ownership, independence conditions and feedback distinct from model-selected S1 code review. - Evidence: packages/agents/src/builtin.ts, packages/cli/src/chat.ts, packages/cli/src/main.ts, packages/cli/src/executor.ts, packages/cli/src/policy.ts. - Basis: confirmed shipped alternative audit constructor, uncertain qualifying ownership and full return. - Confidence: high for optional reviewer wiring; incomplete for S3 functional attribution. - Caveats: packages/eval, replay tooling and repo CI are offline/development/benchmark surfaces; they cannot supply independent audit ownership of ordinary user sessions. - Claim being audited: correctness/completion of changes made by root coding agent. - Ordinary reporting path: parent agent tool output and task-completion summary. - Complementary access path: separate read-only reviewer child can read workspace files and return structured code findings, conditional on parent delegation. - Independence boundary: child receives different instructions, restricted read-only executor and distinct journal scope; still uses the shared selected inference endpoint and voluntarily receives scope from root, with no independently attested assignment. - Who acts on findings: parent coding agent may follow the result; no mandatory auditor-to-current-control disposition pathway established.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed feedback cycle from external/future environmental change to a selected future change of the harness&#39;s own capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conversation growth, protocol parse failures and task output errors are current-session conditions, not independently outside-and-then intelligence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and return prospective capability adaptations into operating mode based on environmental analysis; not evidenced. - Decision owner: no separate S4 agent/constructor; runtime applies fixed compaction and retry policies; offline researchers can change model/toolkit outside credited installed session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session compaction, channel fallback, journaling, static skills, optional hooks and offline toolkit/prune experiments. - Closure path: runtime adapts parser/channel for a failed current task and preserves history; does not establish an external/future strategy choice returned into durable capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task recovery and separate model-pruning research do not supply source-proven first-party S4 remit or return at the declared boundary. - Evidence: packages/core/src/compaction.ts, packages/core/src/loop.ts, toolkit/README.md, packages/skills/src/registry.ts. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: explicitly excluded experimental pruning may affect later releases but is not a proven deployed adaptation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct organizational ultimate-policy and identity judgment right/closure inside the first-party user-session architecture. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe tool access, sandbox escape and ambiguous human approval are bounded operational security risks, not themselves S5 identity governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate parent arbitration and return of ultimate system identity/purpose/policy when contested; no first-party path identified. - Decision owner: human operator selects model, task and approvals; deterministic ExecutionPolicy and read-only sandbox enforce limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: policy roots, allowed tools, approval, credentials, sandbox and settings. - Closure path: a tool request is allowed or denied; no separate ultimate organizational policy ruling returns to set identity and constrain ongoing S1/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human permission decisions and config are task-level governance, not evidenced legitimate S5 organizational control. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/policy.ts, packages/cli/src/sandbox.ts, packages/cli/src/settings.ts, packages/cli/src/executor.ts. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: a host organization may govern Motifcode instances externally; those rights are not claimed as first-party here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/645b4ac12b406ef28f5d705ca2e4f179a4227cd8/assessments/motifcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 645b4ac12b406ef28f5d705ca2e4f179a4227cd8\">645b4ac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T17:21:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codexmark/kram\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kram</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codexmark/kram/tree/5f18406709f432dbdb2d6b09da6f140385516366\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f18406709f432dbdb2d6b09da6f140385516366\">5f18406 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded autonomous repository coding/investigation with model-selected tools and subsequent evidence-based decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source state, shell results, test failures, model responses, permission refusals and child observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next read/edit/command/delegation and revise from actual tool or child results. - Decision owner: root and child model actors in first-party agent.Service.runLoop; the LLM provider supplies a completion, not an external agent implementation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party tool registry, gateway client, persistent messages, run budget and segments, verify and approval gates. - Closure path: model → tool → first-party dispatch/results/history → model next decision or task completion; RunTask runs the same loop for children. - Boundary reachability: standard supported binary and delegatetask tool registration/agent service, not a CI-only fixture or outside CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choices about source actions and fixes come from model calls reacting to observations rather than predetermined script nodes. - Evidence: README.md; internal/daemon/agent/agent.go; internal/daemon/tools/delegate.go; internal/daemon/tools/tools.go. - Basis: directly implemented model/tool loop. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: actual success and latency have not been measured in live model inference here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party closed attenuation of concrete interference between multiple independent child S1 coding operations is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent children can access the same workspace, return incompatible patches or depend on one another; the model is instructed to choose independent subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect actual competing work and choose revised scope/order/ownership with changes returned into continuing child S1 operation. - Decision owner: parent model chooses initial independent goals, while delegatetask starts workers in parallel and returns their final answers; it has no specialized conflict sensor or coordination intervention owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-call concurrency semaphore and max depth, isolated child sessions, task result collating and approval constraints. - Closure path: parent-chosen independent tasks → parallel execution → ordered summaries to parent. No shipped specific cross-child conflict detection/claim registry, resolved merge decision or revised child scope return is demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic fan-out and shared summaries can support coordination externally, but mechanical concurrency limits and a statement that tasks should not interfere are not actual adaptive S2 attenuation. - Evidence: internal/daemon/tools/delegate.go; internal/daemon/agent/agent.go; internal/daemon/agent/systemprompt.go. - Basis: bounded source-negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user can ask a model to coordinate tasks, but no first-party guaranteed concrete interference path closes as an organizational function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no shipped model-owned whole-current population of child S1 operations with ongoing commitments/priority/reallocation authority is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parent/child turn counts and independent delegated subtasks; costs, approvals and gateway fallbacks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: observe whole current child organization and choose reallocations/cancellation/intervention from current outcomes, returning changed commitments into S1s. - Decision owner: parent model can select initial batch tasks, but each delegatetask executes to completion with fixed per-call semaphore; daemon limits are predetermined. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegated batch limits, depth, child session store and gateway telemetry, run context/approval. - Closure path: one-shot task fan-out → child results → parent next task; not a live team-wide current resource/priority view controlling child agents during their operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a generic parent receiving results and a gateway managing model requests is not automatically S3 supervisory regulation. - Evidence: internal/daemon/tools/delegate.go; internal/daemon/agent/agent.go; internal/daemon/store/search.go. - Basis: bounded source-negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an externally assembled long-lived fleet control plane could add S3, outside this binary&#39;s root task mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently owned complementary audit of root/child claims that returns an independently informed corrective judgment into a current S3 organization is shown. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false verification claims, coding mistakes or misleading child results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect operational evidence through a complementary independent path and cause legitimate corrective action outside ordinary task verification. - Decision owner: ordinary verify gate, trace and optional user check are part of the same normal run, not a separate first-party audit actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verification nudges, durable session transcripts, test commands, CI and reliability diagnostics. - Closure path: failure/verify observations can cause another model turn in the operating S1, but not a complementary independent audit-to-S3 regulation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model self-check and human approval are normal execution quality control, not distinct independent S3 governance. - Evidence: internal/daemon/agent/agent.go; internal/daemon/agent/verifygate.go; README.md. - Basis: bounded source-negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: configured specialized external audit could be created separately, but cannot be imported as first-party operational ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective outside-environment sensing with autonomous adaptation of Kram&#39;s future organizational capabilities is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing providers, coding practices and tools across sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose future capabilities from external intelligence and reintroduce the selected change to currently functioning runtime. - Decision owner: user/maintainers choose installed skills/provider config/releases; memory observations support tasks but do not own future strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory/session search, skillpack, provider health/fallback and architecture decisions. - Closure path: current observations become future prompt context, or provider fallback is selected for availability; no prospective strategy selection and capability promotion is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: provider redundancy and memory compaction optimize current work, not forward-looking outside organizational intelligence. - Evidence: internal/daemon/agent/agent.go; internal/daemon/store/search.go; README.md. - Basis: bounded source-negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: changes from manual project maintenance are beyond deployed S4 scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate shared identity/policy dispute resolution function within the first-party Kram runtime is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, approval prompts, provider access and operator-defined project boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide legitimate ultimate identity/mission tradeoffs and return binding policy updates into operation. - Decision owner: operator controls permissions and model credentials; Kram enforces those instructions, not organizational ultimate values. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool allow/deny/ask, permission presets, controlled shell, depth and provider settings. - Closure path: operator-configured rights → tool execution veto or approval → continuation/stop, without ultimate organizational policy adjudication and returned changed identity rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: one permission grant or user approval is operational authorization, not the VSM S5 function. - Evidence: internal/daemon/tools/tools.go; internal/daemon/agent/agent.go; README.md. - Basis: bounded source-negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Kram can be used in an organization that has an independent S5 parent; its human governance is not credited to the binary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/587852cf7b3366e65593f0ab1e2c3e277e28a371/assessments/kram.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 587852cf7b3366e65593f0ab1e2c3e277e28a371\">587852c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T19:42:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/imjiaoyuan/llm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">llm</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/imjiaoyuan/llm/tree/fa532244bc543a02850151a692feca74b15f713c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fa532244bc543a02850151a692feca74b15f713c\">fa53224 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding work by selecting and revising repository inspection, edits, writes, command execution and search actions from current evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, incomplete task requirements, changing file state, command/build/test failures, provider/context errors, oversized results, interrupted streams and user steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next coding/tool action, interpret returned evidence and errors, revise the plan/action sequence, and stop when the model judges the task complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed llm coding agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runagent, built-in tool registry, updateplan, session persistence, compaction/recovery, steering queue, approvals/blacklist, project prompt context and skill discovery. - Closure path: user/project/session context → model chooses tool/action → tool/runtime returns result/error → evidence enters later model round → subsequent coding behaviour changes until completion, interruption or bounded failure. - Boundary reachability: ordinary llm &quot;task&quot; and interactive llm directly instantiate this first-party model/tool loop without requiring an example extension or external orchestrator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves tools, persistence, approvals and rendering but removes the open-ended choice that converts repository evidence into coding actions. - Evidence: README; src/agent/mod.rs; src/agent/repl.rs; src/agent/session.rs; src/agent/tools/; src/agent/systemprompt.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference is external, but the product concretely owns the coding-loop composition and tool feedback path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 coordination function is established in the standard runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one focal coding run may invoke several tools, load extensions or be driven by an external supervisor, but the default product does not expose distinct peer S1 units with a specific mutual interference relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decision over conflict, oscillation or mutual adjustment among distinct S1 units was found. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential tool rounds, approval gating, steering queue, session state and optional extension loading. - Closure path: these mechanisms serialize or constrain one run; no coordination result is fed back into distinct peer operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing and one-agent planning are S1 mechanisms, not S2. - Evidence: src/agent/mod.rs; src/agent/session.rs; src/agent/ext/; README. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: examples/extensions/subagent.py can construct child-agent batches/chains after installation, but that optional example is not an automatically active standard path and, by itself, parallel delegation still would not prove an inter-S1 attenuation witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established beyond local control of one coding run/session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: approvals, interruption, compaction thresholds, session state, tool restrictions and model selection constrain the current run. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate owner has a whole-system current view plus authority over a portfolio of shared operations, priorities, resources or commitments. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session status, interrupt/steering, approvals, tool allowlists, compaction/recovery, model selection and persisted thread state. - Closure path: these controls alter the same focal coding loop and its local session, not a metasystemic current-management loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coding model manages its own task as S1 while users/runtime enforce local controls; neither constitutes distinct whole-system S3. - Evidence: README; src/agent/mod.rs; src/agent/repl.rs; src/agent/session.rs; src/agent/approval.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: --json is explicitly usable by an editor, CI lane or another agent, but that supervising process is external and cannot donate S3 to this harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established in the standard runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bash/tests, tool errors, user steering and optional hooks can reveal defects during coding, but they are ordinary producer feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate first-party reviewer in the default runtime owns an independent claim-checking judgment and returns findings through a distinct current-control path. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell/test execution, tool results, extension hooks and optional reviewer example. - Closure path: default verification evidence returns directly to the same coding actor or user; no separate complementary audit loop is automatically instantiated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-testing inside the coding loop is not sufficiently independent; the shipped reviewer definition is an example requiring separate composition. - Evidence: README; src/agent/tools/bash.rs; src/agent/ext/; examples/agents/reviewer.md; examples/extensions/subagent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can install the first-party example subagent extension and explicitly run the read-only reviewer, but the frozen standard distribution does not wire that reviewer into an ordinary producer→audit→control closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct outside-and-then adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project instructions, skills, packages, extensions, model/provider settings and persisted session context can change what future runs know or can do. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party process senses external/future change, develops adaptation options for the harness and autonomously returns a selected capability change into later operation. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS/CLAUDE project context, skill discovery, package install/refresh, extension loading, session persistence and configurable providers/models. - Closure path: operator-authored or installed capabilities are loaded into later prompts/tools; the runtime does not itself close a prospective environmental adaptation cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the system prompt documents how an agent could write an extension or skill, but that is ordinary current-task extensibility, not a distinct process that senses future environmental change and chooses an adaptation option. - Evidence: README; src/agent/skills.rs; src/agent/systemprompt.rs; src/agent/ext/; package/install paths. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user/project packages can materially expand future capability, but installation/configuration alone is not S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: hard command refusals, blacklist rules, per-tool allow/deny/prompt settings, approval responses and system/project prompt hierarchy constrain ordinary operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a durable governing system decision. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hardcoded refusal set, user/project blacklist, tool approval config, system prompt/project instructions and model/provider settings. - Closure path: safety/configuration decisions constrain current tool execution or future local configuration but do not close an identity-level policy matter for the viable system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static/reflexive safety constraints and user approvals are operational policy enforcement, not S5. - Evidence: README; src/agent/approval.rs; src/agent/blacklist.rs; src/agent/settings.rs; src/agent/systemprompt.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users and maintainers retain real authority over installed packages/configuration, but adjacent operator/development governance is not first-party S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/870e3694f35950c82637e5d315e8a638cde34222/assessments/llm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 870e3694f35950c82637e5d315e8a638cde34222\">870e369 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T22:04:09Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andrewgolovanov/agent-os\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent OS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-01\">2026-10-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andrewgolovanov/agent-os/tree/9f45e60058b51bc69ea746876b798f995d8bebc2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9f45e60058b51bc69ea746876b798f995d8bebc2\">9f45e60 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive registered-project work in a supported Codex task while maintaining one durable Agent OS outcome across turns/tasks and returning material checkpoints into the continuity layer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository/project state, changing implementation/investigation conditions, blockers, exact source context and the risk that important current state disappears when a Codex turn or task ends. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise the next task-specific project action from user objective, project evidence and returned tool/environment state. - Decision owner: the autonomous Codex work cell running with the Agent OS plugin/hooks loaded. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task Board outcomes, exact project registry, Task Bridge membership, plugin hooks, MCP/runtime tools, checkpoint requirement, durable summary/next action, activity history and Codex handoff. - Closure path: registered Codex task receives/claims exact outcome → agent inspects and acts in the project → material mutation triggers Agent OS activity state → agent records verified summary/next action/status checkpoint → Stop closes the turn while preserving the durable outcome → later linked work resumes from that state. - Boundary reachability: the official Agent OS plugin ships the Task Bridge hooks and MCP/runtime bundle and is installed directly into supported Codex; no application-authored orchestration is required to obtain the continuity loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the autonomous Codex worker while leaving registry, Board, hooks and app leaves durable state and enforcement but no actor making the open-ended project decisions. The agent therefore owns S1 discretion while Agent OS supplies the first-party operating envelope. - Evidence: README.md; docs/task-bridge.md; AGENTS.md; plugins/agent-os/hooks/hooks.json. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Codex model reasoning, internal delegation and tool-loop semantics remain external and are not inherited by Agent OS.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 mutual-adjustment loop is established for multiple Agent OS-linked Codex work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible collisions or dependencies among concurrent Codex tasks linked to the same project/outcome were inspected. - Distinct S1 units: multiple independent Codex project tasks may be peer memberships of one outcome or operate on different outcomes/projects. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous work can in principle overlap in repository scope or produce incompatible current assumptions, but no Agent OS-specific contention relation closes over those worker cells. - Attenuating coordination relation: none established beyond exact identity/membership bookkeeping and storage-level file locking. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: no first-party peer-conflict signal, lease/claim refusal, negotiation or mutual-adjustment response path was found that changes one Codex cell because of another cell&#39;s interfering action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: shared Task Board state, one-current-outcome membership and file locking preserve correlation/storage integrity; they do not attenuate concrete inter-worker variety between engineering cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2 at this boundary. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exact task/outcome membership, registry resolution, atomic Task Board writes, routing-pending records, durable history and per-turn activity state. - Closure path: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination closure exists at the frozen ref. - Boundary reachability: the reviewed standard distribution exposes shared outcome state but not an inter-agent conflict/coordination protocol. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents can independently read/update shared outcomes, but no defined Agent OS mechanism requires them to mutually adjust around detected peer interference. - Evidence: docs/task-board.md; docs/task-bridge.md; docs/architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository/Git or project-specific conventions outside Agent OS may coordinate workers, but those are not imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the current Agent OS work portfolio as a whole by deciding what needs attention now, changing outcome lifecycle/next action, selecting the exact Codex continuation path and explicitly closing work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current mismatch across active/waiting/review outcomes, blockers, unfinished next actions, linked Codex tasks and project-level attention competing for the user&#39;s finite current-control capacity. - Whole-system current view: Focus and Board present unfinished outcomes across registered projects/lifecycle states; the inspector exposes goal, summary, next action, blocker, sources, memberships, history and current status from the same canonical Task Board. - Current-control decision scope: choose the current outcome to attend, move lifecycle among active/waiting/review/done/cancelled as appropriate, revise next action/blocker, continue an exact task or create a named handoff, and confirm final completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/revise those current portfolio commitments and interventions rather than merely execute one project-local task. - Decision owner: parent human user/operator through the native app, Task Board commands or equivalent plugin interaction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Focus/Board/Done views, canonical outcome lifecycle, Task Board validated mutations, exact Codex memberships, continuation prompt/handoff, explicit completion confirmation and durable history. - Closure path: shared current portfolio state is projected → human selects/changes priority/lifecycle/handoff → Agent OS writes canonical outcome state or opens the selected Codex continuation → subsequent S1 work follows the updated current-control decision. - Boundary reachability: the native app and Task Board are supported first-party surfaces over the same private runtime and require no custom application orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task Bridge may automatically activate exact-linked work and enforce checkpoints, but it does not autonomously decide which current outcome the installation should prioritize, suspend, resume, hand off or finally close. Those whole-system decisions remain with the user. - Evidence: apps/agent-os/README.md; docs/agent-os-app.md; docs/task-board.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: local lifecycle automation inside one outcome is not independently promoted to S3; the positive mapping is specifically the parent operator&#39;s whole-installation current-control loop.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary independent audit/challenge loop is established in the standard Agent OS operating boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible uncertainty in a Codex work cell&#39;s completion/status claim, repository result, checkpoint or integration health was inspected. - Claim being audited: project work state or readiness as represented by the active work cell/outcome. - Ordinary reporting path: the linked Codex cell records its own verified summary, next action and lifecycle checkpoint; Task Board persists those facts. - Complementary access path: app PR context, history and Setup health can expose additional facts to the human, but no independent first-party auditor separately judges the S1 claim and returns findings into a corrective loop. - Independence boundary: no separate audit actor or mechanically independent challenge relation meeting S3 closure was found. - Who acts on findings: the parent user may inspect context and choose further work, but that is ordinary S3 parent control rather than a distinct complementary audit subsystem. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3 at the deployed boundary. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: checkpoints, event history, optional GitHub PR read context, Setup capability/health observations, explicit completion confirmation and validation commands. - Closure path: no first-party complementary audit judgment → corrective return → re-audit loop is established. - Boundary reachability: standard app/plugin surfaces expose evidence but not a separate auditor role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: requiring the same work cell to checkpoint after its own mutation is ordinary self-report/continuity, not complementary audit. Human inspection belongs to the mapped parent S3 path unless a distinct audit function is closed. - Evidence: docs/task-bridge.md; docs/agent-os-app.md; docs/task-board.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external CI, repository reviewers or Codex behaviors could provide audit outside this first-party boundary and are not inherited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established in the reviewed Agent OS standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Slack signals, provider availability, project changes, software versions, schedules and possible future work conditions were inspected as candidate S4 inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that turns external observations into a model of future environment, develops adaptation options and returns an adaptation choice into current Agent OS capability/policy. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional Slack monitor, recurring schedule intent, current project synchronization, version/update checks, Task Board follow-up and notifications. - Closure path: no supported outside-and-then intelligence/adaptation decision-and-return loop exists at the frozen ref. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Slack monitor repeatedly senses current unresolved asks and converts actionable present signals into local outcomes; scheduled repetition does not make it prospective intelligence. Project/version discovery likewise reacts to current observed state rather than developing future adaptation options. - Evidence: docs/slack-monitor.md; docs/optional-integrations.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/agent-os-app.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: recurring review/monitor execution can create current work from newly observed external events, but no prospective adaptation function is closed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material ultimate organizational identity/policy closure is established for the Agent OS installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible conflicts over project identity, trust, external-write authority, plugin/schedule approval and highest-level operating policy were inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established as an S5-specific runtime path beyond ordinary user configuration/consent, exact object identity and current-control decisions. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this deployed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stable project/source/task identities, preview-first changes, explicit user intent for external writes, plugin trust review, separate integration authorization, completion confirmation and update opt-ins. - Closure path: no first-party path was found where an organizational identity/ultimate-policy issue is escalated to legitimate ultimate authority and the returned decision governs subsequent Agent OS operation as S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project keys, exact source identities and permission/approval gates are operational identity and authorization mechanisms, not a closure over organizational purpose/constitution. Human consent alone is not promoted to parent S5 without the S5-specific escalation/return relation. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; docs/task-board.md; docs/optional-integrations.md; AGENTS.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository/product governance and user-owned project rules may embody policy outside the selected deployed organization but are not borrowed as Agent OS S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e6accae74e11d0b03fb48b4edd3f12668ec7b5ca/assessments/agent-os.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e6accae74e11d0b03fb48b4edd3f12668ec7b5ca\">e6accae &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T20:16:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Idkwhattonametsbro/autonomous-cloud-workforce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Autonomous Cloud Workforce</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Idkwhattonametsbro/autonomous-cloud-workforce/tree/83d06d6dafef9f6d08e92c596d139515e987ae87\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 83d06d6dafef9f6d08e92c596d139515e987ae87\">83d06d6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: The first-party ReAct actor interprets a goal, chooses task-specific actions and tool arguments, observes external responses, and continues toward requested operational outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable inbox contents, search/API/file observations, incomplete user requests, failed tool attempts and provider uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: For each task iteration choose the next function call or completed response after receiving the previous observation. - Decision owner: A model queried through the first-party ModelRouter by ReActAgent. The runtime executes chosen tools, but the discretionary choice of next task action belongs to the model-backed actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Configured Groq/OpenRouter LLM APIs and fallback roles, JSON tool schemas, ToolRegistry handlers, bounded maxreactiterations, conversation history, logging, GitHub Actions timeouts and persistence. - Closure path: main.py/Actions or dashboard receives a goal → ReActAgent.run initializes prompt/history → thinkanddecide uses ModelRouter.query with function schema → executetools invokes selected handler → observe adds assistant call and tool result to model conversation → next iteration chooses response to observed environment → TASKCOMPLETE or iteration limit. Provider errors also enter history for a subsequent model decision. - Boundary reachability: At pinned source, main.py calls config.validate, createdefaultregistry and ReActAgent.run; Actions installs requirements and runs main.py, while the dashboard calls the same ReActAgent for real-key mode. ModelRouter issues native function-calling SDK/HTTP requests and tool calls are fed back to it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: If the model actor is removed while deterministic scheduling, tools, persistent reports and dashboard remain, there is no first-party discretionary selection of task actions from observations. The first-party ReAct loop is therefore an S1 task actor; external inference execution is a configured implementation dependency rather than an unrelated external agent CLI. - Evidence: src/agent.py; src/models.py; src/tools.py; main.py; .github/workflows/agent.yml; src/config.py; requirements.txt - Basis: Explicit code + structurally wired model/tool feedback - Confidence: High for first-party code path; no actual remote inference performed. - Caveats: The live credential-enabled mode is distinct from dashboard automatic demo fallback; documented product results are not verified. The evaluator VM omitted installed requirements (requests), though requirements.txt and Actions install declare them.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No source-evidenced attenuation of actual reciprocal interference among independently continuing viable operational S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping task requests, tool collisions and multiple dashboard agent instances might interfere with shared credentials, data or external services. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The dashboard can spawn ReActAgent instances and collect separate status/event streams; Actions concurrency serializes runs. It does not assign a coordinating controller that detects, arbitrates and feeds back actual inter-S1 conflict to change both continuing operating units. - Decision owner: Operator-selected goals, deterministic dashboard state/runs and GitHub Actions concurrency (support mechanisms, not S2 autonomy). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dashboard AgentInstance registry/RLock, event callbacks, socket streams, /api/agents endpoints, Actions concurrency group and per-instance tool histories. - Closure path: POST /api/agents creates a labeled instance → POST /api/start launches an independent thread → ReActAgent acts on its own goal → per-agent events/status shown. Missing: units A and B create disturbance → designated S2 detects/attenuates oscillation → balancing decision returns to both future operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple processes or agent instances are not sufficient evidence of S2. There is no first-party coordination relation addressing real mutual disturbance between independently viable S1s. Cron serialization is scheduling rather than homeostatic coordination. - Evidence: dashboard/app.py; src/agent.py; .github/workflows/agent.yml; src/infrastructure.py - Basis: Source-checked bounded negative - Confidence: High for supported cron/dashboard paths. - Caveats: A custom downstream operator could externally coordinate tasks but is not credited as a shipped first-party organizational function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party accountable present-organization management of a continuing portfolio of independently viable S1 divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent goals, tool failures, costs, duration overruns, rate limits and operator allocation priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A human uses dashboard endpoints to start agents and inspect status, while hard-coded configuration and circuit breaker support limit usage. No managerial agent decides whole-organization allocations, reconciles competing ongoing S1 commitments and returns enforceable cross-unit operating adjustments. - Decision owner: Externally deciding user plus deterministic UI/API and task-loop configuration. Neither is a shipped S3 discretionary governance actor or a closed S3 parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent registry, resource monitor, CircuitBreaker, RateLimiter, CostTracker, AuditLog, analytics, Run reports and per-run concurrency. - Closure path: Dashboard user requests agent spawn/start → thread runs one goal → emits status, report and metrics. No whole-current S1-managerial report → manager binding allocation/portfolio correction → changed operational unit constraints across continuing businesses. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The worker model decides task-level steps and the user chooses tasks. UI state, resource thresholds and budget displays are not an internally delegated ongoing manager. Parent-mode checks find no operational contract returning authoritative S3 management corrections across independent operational units. - Evidence: dashboard/app.py; src/infrastructure.py; src/agent.py; dashboard/analytics.py; main.py - Basis: Bounded absence after inspecting both deterministic runtime and parent-facing dashboard - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A real company may use the dashboards as a management aid; its managerial authority remains external.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No management-facing independent direct-evidence audit correcting an S3 operational account. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent could incorrectly report success, overstate progress, use unsafe tools or produce misleading logs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Tool results are logged, interactions stored and a dashboard analyzer scores report statistics; none independently samples actual S1 operating facts to challenge an existing whole-system S3 management report and produce binding corrective governance feedback. - Decision owner: Deterministic analytics and optional analyzer logic; no independent direct-evidence audit role with mandate/authority over S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool execution event reports, AuditLog, dashboard AnalyticsStore, self-improvement summaries, tool error rates and run histories. - Closure path: ReActAgent emits event/history → dashboard records report → SelfImprover computes efficiency/tool error findings → memory stores strategy/status for display; there is no independently acquired evidence contradiciting a S3 manager&#39;s claims and returned S3 corrective decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent tool observation is part of S1 control; independent run metrics/QA do not automatically become complementary organizational audit without a corresponding S3 picture and correction right. - Evidence: src/agent.py; dashboard/app.py; dashboard/selfimprove.py; dashboard/analytics.py; src/infrastructure.py - Basis: Bounded source-backed negative - Confidence: High at assessed recursion. - Caveats: Reported tool statuses and self-reported TASKCOMPLETE should not be read as verified task success.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No self-sustaining future-oriented organizational strategic adaptation loop that changes subsequent task-agent capability policy based on environmental intelligence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future tool reliability changes, model availability, repeated task mistakes and broader mission opportunities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: SelfImprover.analyzerun computes a post-run strategy list, remembers laststrategy/runhistory/successrate and displays summaries through dashboard; source doesn&#39;t route laststrategy into ReActAgent prompts/tool routing or subsequent scheduled main.py runs. AdvancedIntelligence and AdvancedMemory offer services not integrated into baseline ReAct loop. - Decision owner: Deterministic diagnostic analytics and external user dashboard interpretation, not an agent-adopted future organizational capability owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SelfImprover, advanced memory episodic records, performance metrics, model config and status APIs; external manual setting changes. - Closure path: Run report+events → analyzer computes avoidtools / recommended iterations / strategy → persistent memory and status endpoint. Missing closure: strategy adopted by authoritative decision maker → modified future actor configuration or operating policy → verified changed behavior. Main ReActAgent and scheduled main.py do not retrieve the stored strategy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistent self-improvement data plus a class named SelfImprover is a plausible S4 candidate, but there is no evidence-backed strategy adoption and operational return. Per-task failure learning is S1 correction, not the future organizational outside-and-then function. - Evidence: dashboard/selfimprove.py; dashboard/app.py; src/agent.py; main.py; src/intelligence.py; src/memoryadvanced.py; src/config.py - Basis: Strong plausible candidate explicitly checked; bounded negative - Confidence: High for lack of returned adoption in first-party ordinary deployment. - Caveats: An operator can read recommendations and improve source/config outside this frozen package; that&#39;s not a shipped parent-governed closed S4 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No whole-organization identity/purpose arbitration authority that can resolve S3/S4 tension and return constitutional rules to continuing S1 operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting goals, potentially sensitive external services, connector permissions, API credential constraints and execution-time budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user supplies AGENTGOAL and credentials; system prompts prescribe current action rules, dashboard stores agentname/version identity fields, secrets/tools return needsconnection; these are task identity and configured safety constraints rather than S5 ultimate-policy adjudication. - Decision owner: External operator/settings and static script policy; no first-party autonomous S5/legitimate parent constitutional deliberative mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: REACTSYSTEMPROMPT, AppConfig, max iterations, secret-bound tool checks, dashboard static identity records, workflow permissions and timeout, logging. - Closure path: Parent provides goal/config → task is attempted within configured bounds → success/error returned. No competing present-vs-future organizational priorities → legitimate S5 constitutional judgment → binding purpose/identity revision fed back into subsequent operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The role prompt&#39;s instruction &#39;never ask human&#39; does not grant a system constitutional authority; a dashboard username/identity key is metadata. User changing variables is not itself an S5 parent mode with purpose arbitration and decision return. - Evidence: src/agent.py; src/config.py; dashboard/app.py; .github/workflows/agent.yml; src/tools.py - Basis: Bounded negative with parent-mode scrutiny - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A user/operator can govern an external business identity; outside the source&#39;s autonomous agent organizational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6975d531985d3ad23dbe81790a3c5794f4f0ce3f/assessments/au/autonomous-cloud-workforce.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6975d531985d3ad23dbe81790a3c5794f4f0ce3f\">6975d53 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T19:52:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bigfish1913/pi-rust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rpi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bigfish1913/pi-rust/tree/db4eacd64e8c24939345fb6bd4efc2ef4328ee13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision db4eacd64e8c24939345fb6bd4efc2ef4328ee13\">db4eacd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-guided coding work with observable tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source files, unknown repository topology, build/test outputs, tool errors and task uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next coding/file/shell tool invocation from observed model/tool context and adapt based on returned results. - Decision owner: first-party rpi-agent model-directed coding actor, mediated by runtime; external model supplies inference, not an imported harness runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentBuilder, runagentloop, stream function, tool registry, ExecutionEnv, transcript state and events. - Closure path: user prompt→provider model response→tool calls executed in rpi-tools→tool outcomes appended→next model response or terminal answer. - Boundary reachability: installed rpi-cli and embedded rpi-agent SDK use this shipped first-party actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unbounded task steps and tool choices are model-dependent, not simply a fixed developer script. - Evidence: crates/pi-agent/src/agentloop.rs, crates/pi-agent/src/agent.rs, crates/pi-tools/src/lib.rs, crates/pi-cli/src/agentsession.rs. - Basis: first-party source / installed CLI and SDK. - Confidence: high for source path, no live provider output measured. - Caveats: actual quality/latency and optional third-party tools vary by setup.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no supported interference-specific attenuation between multiple distinct S1 operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel tool calls or different user-embedded agents might edit overlapping files, but no first-party multi-unit conflict feedback is evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect a resource collision between independent operational actors and return a corrected allocation or arbitration; absent. - Decision owner: runtime tool scheduling and queue operators act inside one agent rather than coordinating an independent S1 group. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: single-agent tool execution modes, queues, cancellation and standalone plugin ABI. - Closure path: selected tools execute and report back to the same model; no distinct collision finding→inter-agent changed work path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool scheduling inside one actor and generic pub/sub do not prove the VSM S2 inter-S1 relation. - Evidence: crates/pi-agent/src/agentloop.rs, crates/pi-agent/src/agent.rs, crates/pi-harness/src/runtime.rs. - Basis: bounded negative native-source inspection. - Confidence: medium for the reviewed standard distribution. - Caveats: external embedding or user-installed plugins may change the boundary and require separate assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no supported distinct whole-current regulation of a live multi-S1 work organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: session budget, crashes, user steer/abort and model/tool errors are handled as runtime mechanics. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and enforce whole-system current operational commitments across separately executing S1 units; absent. - Decision owner: single model and user operate session controls, no separate differentiated portfolio supervisor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session recovery, user steering, run budget, CLI status and JSONL event log. - Closure path: stop/resume/steer controls a coding session but no separate whole-current controller redistributes an S1 population. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: resumability and user task control alone do not establish an independent S3 actor. - Evidence: crates/pi-harness/src/session/session.rs, crates/pi-harness/src/agentharness.rs, crates/pi-agent/src/agent.rs. - Basis: bounded negative native-source inspection. - Confidence: medium for the reviewed standard distribution. - Caveats: external embedding or user-installed plugins may change the boundary and require separate assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no supported independently owned evidence-taking review of S1 output claims with binding finding disposition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding errors or invalid final claims can persist if the model does not independently check them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate auditor challenges the producer from privileged evidence and forces correction or explicit failed handoff; absent. - Decision owner: ordinary main model may run tools/tests but no installed independent reviewer is assigned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool results, event stream and searchable session logs, without review-verdict contract. - Closure path: ordinary errors/test results are observations in S1; no separate challenger→finding→mandatory return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: observability, logs and manual test calls do not equal an independent complementary claim audit. - Evidence: crates/pi-agent/src/agentloop.rs, crates/pi-harness/src/session/search.rs, crates/pi-harness/src/events.rs. - Basis: bounded negative native-source inspection. - Confidence: medium for the reviewed standard distribution. - Caveats: external embedding or user-installed plugins may change the boundary and require separate assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported outside/future sensing, capability decision and return to improve later rpi operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model availability, future project patterns or changed user needs are not tracked as separate strategic intelligence here. - Decisive decision or feedback right: change future installed agent capabilities based on independent outside evidence; absent. - Decision owner: user/install manager and external plugins can change capability manually, not a built-in adaptation owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compaction, skills, context files, provider adapters and package/plugin loader. - Closure path: current session remembers context or reloads externally supplied tools; no observed future-facing sensing→option→adoption. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context persistence and an extensible ABI do not prove autonomous long-horizon adaptation. - Evidence: crates/pi-harness/src/compaction/compaction.rs, crates/pi-harness/src/skills.rs, crates/pi-cli/src/packages.rs. - Basis: bounded negative native-source inspection. - Confidence: medium for the reviewed standard distribution. - Caveats: external embedding or user-installed plugins may change the boundary and require separate assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported ultimate system identity/purpose adjudication by a legitimate separate owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider credentials, settings and tool rights are standard execution boundaries rather than constitutional identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret overall purpose and policy authority, return binding identity resolution to S3/S4 and S1; absent. - Decision owner: user config and caller define program instructions; no internal rightful ultimate-policy function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system-prompt setting, provider selection, plugin trust and CLI options. - Closure path: configured system prompt and tool permissions constrain S1 but do not adjudicate a contested higher-level identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a system prompt, user task and config are not an accountable S5 decision organ. - Evidence: crates/pi-harness/src/systemprompt.rs, crates/pi-cli/src/session.rs, crates/pi-agent/src/agent.rs. - Basis: bounded negative native-source inspection. - Confidence: medium for the reviewed standard distribution. - Caveats: external embedding or user-installed plugins may change the boundary and require separate assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f6a2e326b2c212f0123b5386929c4e1653c6193/assessments/rpi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f6a2e326b2c212f0123b5386929c4e1653c6193\">9f6a2e3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
